          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. Give one example each of (a) a solid-in-solid solution, and (b) a gas-in-solid solution.\n2. Why is **molality (m)** preferred over **molarity (M)** in experiments where temperature varies?\n3. Calculate the mass percentage of benzene (C₆H₆) when 22 g of benzene is dissolved in 122 g of carbon tetrachloride (CCl₄).\n4. A 500 g sample of seawater contains 3.0 × 10⁻³ g of dissolved oxygen (O₂). Express the concentration of O₂ in parts per million (ppm).\n5. Calculate the molarity of a solution containing 5.0 g of NaOH (molar mass 40 g/mol) in 500 mL of solution.\n6. Calculate the molality of 2.5 g of ethanoic acid (CH₃COOH, molar mass 60 g/mol) dissolved in 75 g of benzene.\n7. A solution contains 9.0 g of glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆, molar mass 180 g/mol) dissolved in 36 g of water (molar mass 18 g/mol). Find the mole fraction of glucose and water.\n8. State whether True or False: The sum of mole fractions of all components in any solution is always equal to 1.\n9. What is the molarity of pure water (density = 1.0 g/mL, molar mass = 18 g/mol)?\n10. A 10% (w/w) aqueous glucose solution has a density of 1.2 g/mL. Calculate its (a) molality, and (b) molarity (molar mass of glucose = 180 g/mol).\n\n### Answers\n\n1. (a) **Brass** (Zinc in Copper) ya **22-carat Gold** (Copper in Gold); (b) **Hydrogen gas in Palladium (Pd)**.\n\n2. Molality sirf **mass** par depend karti hai (jo temperature se nahi badalta), jabki molarity **volume** par depend karti hai jo temperature badhne par expand hota hai!\n\n3. Total mass = 22 + 122 = 144 g.\n   Mass % of benzene = (22 / 144) × 100 ≈ **15.28%**.\n   Jaanch: 22 / 144 ≈ 0.1528 ✅.\n\n4. ppm = (3.0 × 10⁻³ / 500) × 10⁶ = (3.0 × 10³) / 500 = **6.0 ppm**.\n   Jaanch: 3000 / 500 = 6.0 ppm ✅.\n\n5. n = 5.0 / 40 = 0.125 mol; V = 0.50 L:\n   **M = 0.125 / 0.50 = 0.25 M**.\n   Jaanch: 0.25 × 0.50 = 0.125 mol ✅.\n\n6. n = 2.5 / 60 = 1/24 mol; W_solvent = 75 g = 0.075 kg:\n   **m = (1/24) / 0.075 = 1 / 1.8 ≈ 0.556 mol/kg**.\n   Jaanch: 24 × 0.075 = 1.8; 1/1.8 = 5/9 ≈ 0.556 ✅.\n\n7. n_glucose = 9.0 / 180 = 0.05 mol; n_water = 36 / 18 = 2.0 mol; total = 2.05 mol.\n   **x_glucose = 0.05 / 2.05 = 1/41 ≈ 0.0244**; **x_water = 1 − 0.0244 = 0.9756**.\n   Jaanch: 0.0244 + 0.9756 = 1.000 ✅.\n\n8. **True ✅.** x₁ + x₂ + ... = (n₁ + n₂ + ...) / n_total = 1.\n\n9. 1 L (1000 mL) pure water = 1000 g ⟹ n = 1000 / 18 ≈ **55.56 mol** ⟹ **M = 55.56 M**.\n   Jaanch: 1000 / 18 = 55.555... ✅.\n\n10. 100 g solution mein: 10 g glucose (n = 10/180 = 1/18 mol), 90 g water (0.090 kg).\n    (a) **m = (1/18) / 0.090 = 1 / 1.62 ≈ 0.617 mol/kg**.\n    (b) Volume of 100 g solution = 100 / 1.2 = 83.33 mL = 0.08333 L:\n        **M = (1/18) / (1/12) = 12 / 18 = 2/3 ≈ 0.667 mol/L**.\n    Jaanch: 100 / 1.2 mL = 1/12 L; (1/18)/(1/12) = 2/3 = 0.667 M ✅.\n\n---\n\nAb tum sabhi Types of Solutions aur Concentration Units ko master kar chuke ho!\n\n**Agle topic mein:** Thandi cola zyada fizzy kyun hoti hai, aur samundar ki gahrai mein scuba divers ko **Nitrogen Bends** se kaise bachaya jaata hai — seekhenge **Solubility aur Henry's Law**!",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. Give one example each of (a) a solid-in-solid solution, and (b) a gas-in-solid solution.\n2. Why is **molality (m)** preferred over **molarity (M)** in experiments where temperature varies?\n3. Calculate the mass percentage of benzene (C₆H₆) when 22 g of benzene is dissolved in 122 g of carbon tetrachloride (CCl₄).\n4. A 500 g sample of seawater contains 3.0 × 10⁻³ g of dissolved oxygen (O₂). Express the concentration of O₂ in parts per million (ppm).\n5. Calculate the molarity of a solution containing 5.0 g of NaOH (molar mass 40 g/mol) in 500 mL of solution.\n6. Calculate the molality of 2.5 g of ethanoic acid (CH₃COOH, molar mass 60 g/mol) dissolved in 75 g of benzene.\n7. A solution contains 9.0 g of glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆, molar mass 180 g/mol) dissolved in 36 g of water (molar mass 18 g/mol). Find the mole fraction of glucose and water.\n8. State whether True or False: The sum of mole fractions of all components in any solution is always equal to 1.\n9. What is the molarity of pure water (density = 1.0 g/mL, molar mass = 18 g/mol)?\n10. A 10% (w/w) aqueous glucose solution has a density of 1.2 g/mL. Calculate its (a) molality, and (b) molarity (molar mass of glucose = 180 g/mol).\n\n### Answers\n\n1. (a) **Brass** (Zinc in Copper) ya **22-carat Gold** (Copper in Gold); (b) **Hydrogen gas in Palladium (Pd)**.\n\n2. Molality sirf **mass** par depend karti hai (jo temperature se nahi badalta), jabki molarity **volume** par depend karti hai jo temperature badhne par expand hota hai!\n\n3. Total mass = 22 + 122 = 144 g.\n   Mass % of benzene = (22 / 144) × 100 ≈ **15.28%**.\n   Jaanch: 22 / 144 ≈ 0.1528 ✅.\n\n4. ppm = (3.0 × 10⁻³ / 500) × 10⁶ = (3.0 × 10³) / 500 = **6.0 ppm**.\n   Jaanch: 3000 / 500 = 6.0 ppm ✅.\n\n5. n = 5.0 / 40 = 0.125 mol; V = 0.50 L:\n   **M = 0.125 / 0.50 = 0.25 M**.\n   Jaanch: 0.25 × 0.50 = 0.125 mol ✅.\n\n6. n = 2.5 / 60 = 1/24 mol; W_solvent = 75 g = 0.075 kg:\n   **m = (1/24) / 0.075 = 1 / 1.8 ≈ 0.556 mol/kg**.\n   Jaanch: 24 × 0.075 = 1.8; 1/1.8 = 5/9 ≈ 0.556 ✅.\n\n7. n_glucose = 9.0 / 180 = 0.05 mol; n_water = 36 / 18 = 2.0 mol; total = 2.05 mol.\n   **x_glucose = 0.05 / 2.05 = 1/41 ≈ 0.0244**; **x_water = 1 − 0.0244 = 0.9756**.\n   Jaanch: 0.0244 + 0.9756 = 1.000 ✅.\n\n8. **True ✅.** x₁ + x₂ + ... = (n₁ + n₂ + ...) / n_total = 1.\n\n9. 1 L (1000 mL) pure water = 1000 g ⟹ n = 1000 / 18 ≈ **55.56 mol** ⟹ **M = 55.56 M**.\n   Jaanch: 1000 / 18 = 55.555... ✅.\n\n10. 100 g solution mein: 10 g glucose (n = 10/180 = 1/18 mol), 90 g water (0.090 kg).\n    (a) **m = (1/18) / 0.090 = 1 / 1.62 ≈ 0.617 mol/kg**.\n    (b) Volume of 100 g solution = 100 / 1.2 = 83.33 mL = 0.08333 L:\n        **M = (1/18) / (1/12) = 12 / 18 = 2/3 ≈ 0.667 mol/L**.\n    Jaanch: 100 / 1.2 mL = 1/12 L; (1/18)/(1/12) = 2/3 = 0.667 M ✅.\n\n---\n\nAb tum sabhi Types of Solutions aur Concentration Units ko master kar chuke ho!\n\n**Agle topic mein:** Thandi cola zyada fizzy kyun hoti hai, aur samundar ki gahrai mein scuba divers ko **Nitrogen Bends** se kaise bachaya jaata hai — seekhenge **Solubility aur Henry's Law**!"
          }
