            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. Write the general electronic configuration of lanthanoids and actinoids.\n2. What is lanthanoid contraction? Explain its underlying cause.\n3. List three important consequences of lanthanoid contraction.\n4. Which lanthanoid element is radioactive?\n5. Why is La(OH)₃ more basic than Lu(OH)₃?\n6. Explain why Eu²⁺ acts as a strong reducing agent whereas Ce⁴⁺ acts as a strong oxidising agent.\n7. What is mischmetall? State its composition and one important use.\n8. Give three points of difference between lanthanoids and actinoids.\n9. Why is actinoid contraction greater from element to element than lanthanoid contraction?\n10. Write the electronic configurations of Gd (Z = 64) and Lu³⁺ (Z = 71).\n\n---\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Lanthanoids: **[Xe] 4f¹⁻¹⁴ 5d⁰⁻¹ 6s²**. Actinoids: **[Rn] 5f¹⁻¹⁴ 6d⁰⁻¹ 7s²**.\n2. **Lanthanoid contraction** is the steady decrease in atomic and ionic radii from La (Z = 57) to Lu (Z = 71). **Cause:** As nuclear charge increases across the series, new electrons enter the diffuse 4f orbitals which offer very poor shielding against the nuclear pull.\n3. (i) Almost identical radii of 4d and 5d transition pairs (e.g., Zr ≈ Hf), making their separation difficult. (ii) Decrease in basic strength of hydroxides from La(OH)₃ to Lu(OH)₃. (iii) Very similar chemical properties of lanthanoids, requiring ion-exchange methods for separation.\n4. **Promethium (Pm, Z = 61)** is the only radioactive lanthanoid.\n5. Because Lu³⁺ is smaller than La³⁺ (lanthanoid contraction), the Lu–OH bond has greater covalent character (Fajans' rule) and releases OH⁻ less easily than the more ionic La–OH bond.\n6. Eu²⁺ ([Xe] 4f⁷) tends to oxidise to the most stable lanthanoid state Eu³⁺ (strong reducing agent). Ce⁴⁺ ([Xe] 4f⁰) tends to reduce to the more stable aqueous state Ce³⁺ (strong oxidising agent).\n7. **Mischmetall** is an alloy of ~95% lanthanoid metals, ~5% iron, and traces of S, C, Ca, and Al. It is used in magnesium-based alloys for bullets, shells, and lighter flints.\n8. (i) Lanthanoids are non-radioactive (except Pm); all actinoids are radioactive. (ii) Lanthanoids show mainly +3 state; actinoids show wide states (+3 to +7). (iii) Lanthanoids do not form oxo-cations; actinoids form oxo-cations like UO₂²⁺.\n9. Because **5f electrons** are even more diffuse and offer **poorer shielding** of the nuclear charge than 4f electrons.\n10. - **Gd (Z = 64):** **[Xe] 4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s²** (half-filled 4f⁷ stability).\n    - **Lu³⁺ (Z = 71, 68 e⁻):** **[Xe] 4f¹⁴** (5d¹ and 6s² removed).\n    Jaanch: Gd = 54 + 7 + 1 + 2 = 64; Lu³⁺ = 54 + 14 = 68 = 71 − 3 ✅\n\n---\n\nAb tumne f-block ke lanthanoids, actinoids aur contraction effects ko bilkul crystal clear samajh liya hai!\n\n**Agle topic mein:** [Sab Kuch Mila Kar: Mixed Problems & Chapter Wrap-up](the-d-and-f-block-elements-part5.md) mein hum poore d- and f-block chapter ke mixed conceptual aur numerical questions solve karenge aur final summary table banayenge!",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. Write the general electronic configuration of lanthanoids and actinoids.\n2. What is lanthanoid contraction? Explain its underlying cause.\n3. List three important consequences of lanthanoid contraction.\n4. Which lanthanoid element is radioactive?\n5. Why is La(OH)₃ more basic than Lu(OH)₃?\n6. Explain why Eu²⁺ acts as a strong reducing agent whereas Ce⁴⁺ acts as a strong oxidising agent.\n7. What is mischmetall? State its composition and one important use.\n8. Give three points of difference between lanthanoids and actinoids.\n9. Why is actinoid contraction greater from element to element than lanthanoid contraction?\n10. Write the electronic configurations of Gd (Z = 64) and Lu³⁺ (Z = 71).\n\n---\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Lanthanoids: **[Xe] 4f¹⁻¹⁴ 5d⁰⁻¹ 6s²**. Actinoids: **[Rn] 5f¹⁻¹⁴ 6d⁰⁻¹ 7s²**.\n2. **Lanthanoid contraction** is the steady decrease in atomic and ionic radii from La (Z = 57) to Lu (Z = 71). **Cause:** As nuclear charge increases across the series, new electrons enter the diffuse 4f orbitals which offer very poor shielding against the nuclear pull.\n3. (i) Almost identical radii of 4d and 5d transition pairs (e.g., Zr ≈ Hf), making their separation difficult. (ii) Decrease in basic strength of hydroxides from La(OH)₃ to Lu(OH)₃. (iii) Very similar chemical properties of lanthanoids, requiring ion-exchange methods for separation.\n4. **Promethium (Pm, Z = 61)** is the only radioactive lanthanoid.\n5. Because Lu³⁺ is smaller than La³⁺ (lanthanoid contraction), the Lu–OH bond has greater covalent character (Fajans' rule) and releases OH⁻ less easily than the more ionic La–OH bond.\n6. Eu²⁺ ([Xe] 4f⁷) tends to oxidise to the most stable lanthanoid state Eu³⁺ (strong reducing agent). Ce⁴⁺ ([Xe] 4f⁰) tends to reduce to the more stable aqueous state Ce³⁺ (strong oxidising agent).\n7. **Mischmetall** is an alloy of ~95% lanthanoid metals, ~5% iron, and traces of S, C, Ca, and Al. It is used in magnesium-based alloys for bullets, shells, and lighter flints.\n8. (i) Lanthanoids are non-radioactive (except Pm); all actinoids are radioactive. (ii) Lanthanoids show mainly +3 state; actinoids show wide states (+3 to +7). (iii) Lanthanoids do not form oxo-cations; actinoids form oxo-cations like UO₂²⁺.\n9. Because **5f electrons** are even more diffuse and offer **poorer shielding** of the nuclear charge than 4f electrons.\n10. - **Gd (Z = 64):** **[Xe] 4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s²** (half-filled 4f⁷ stability).\n    - **Lu³⁺ (Z = 71, 68 e⁻):** **[Xe] 4f¹⁴** (5d¹ and 6s² removed).\n    Jaanch: Gd = 54 + 7 + 1 + 2 = 64; Lu³⁺ = 54 + 14 = 68 = 71 − 3 ✅\n\n---\n\nAb tumne f-block ke lanthanoids, actinoids aur contraction effects ko bilkul crystal clear samajh liya hai!\n\n**Agle topic mein:** [Sab Kuch Mila Kar: Mixed Problems & Chapter Wrap-up](the-d-and-f-block-elements-part5.md) mein hum poore d- and f-block chapter ke mixed conceptual aur numerical questions solve karenge aur final summary table banayenge!"
