          {
            "heading": "1. Bonding in Metal Carbonyls (Synergic Bonding)",
            "headingHinglish": "1. Bonding in Metal Carbonyls (Synergic Bonding)",
            "body": "> **Safety Note:** Metal carbonyls (jaise Ni(CO)₄) aur carbon monoxide (CO) behad zehreele (highly toxic) hote hain; inka study sirf industrial processes aur theory mein hota hai.\n\n### Common Metal Carbonyl Structures:\n- **[Ni(CO)₄]:** Tetrahedral (Ni = 0, sp³)\n- **[Fe(CO)₅]:** Trigonal bipyramidal (Fe = 0, dsp³ / sp³d)\n- **[Cr(CO)₆]:** Octahedral (Cr = 0, d²sp³)\n- **[Mn₂(CO)₁₀]:** Do square-pyramidal Mn(CO)₅ units ek **Mn–Mn single bond** se judi hui (no bridging CO!)\n- **[Co₂(CO)₈]:** Ek **Co–Co bond** + **2 bridging CO** groups + 6 terminal CO groups!\n\n### How Synergic Bonding Works (σ + π Back-Bonding):\nMetal–Carbon (M–C) bond mein **double-bond character** hota hai, jo do steps mein banta hai:\n1. **M ← C σ-bond:** CO ke carbon par maujood lone pair metal ke vacant hybrid orbital mein donate hota hai.\n2. **M → C π-bond (Back-bonding):** Metal ke **filled d-orbital** se ek electron pair CO ke **vacant antibonding π* orbital** mein wapas donate hota hai!\n\n```\nSynergic Bonding in M—C≡O\n\n        σ-donation (lone pair)\n      <------------------------\n    M                           C ≡ O\n      ------------------------>\n      π back-donation (filled d → vacant π* of CO)\n```\n\n**Nateeja (Result):**\n- Dono bonds ek-doosre ko mazboot banate hain (**synergic effect**)!\n- **M–C bond mazboot aur chhota** ho jata hai (bond order > 1).\n- **C–O bond thoda kamzor aur lamba** ho jata hai (kyunki electrons CO ke *antibonding* π* orbital mein gaye!).",
            "bodyHinglish": "> **Safety Note:** Metal carbonyls (jaise Ni(CO)₄) aur carbon monoxide (CO) behad zehreele (highly toxic) hote hain; inka study sirf industrial processes aur theory mein hota hai.\n\n### Common Metal Carbonyl Structures:\n- **[Ni(CO)₄]:** Tetrahedral (Ni = 0, sp³)\n- **[Fe(CO)₅]:** Trigonal bipyramidal (Fe = 0, dsp³ / sp³d)\n- **[Cr(CO)₆]:** Octahedral (Cr = 0, d²sp³)\n- **[Mn₂(CO)₁₀]:** Do square-pyramidal Mn(CO)₅ units ek **Mn–Mn single bond** se judi hui (no bridging CO!)\n- **[Co₂(CO)₈]:** Ek **Co–Co bond** + **2 bridging CO** groups + 6 terminal CO groups!\n\n### How Synergic Bonding Works (σ + π Back-Bonding):\nMetal–Carbon (M–C) bond mein **double-bond character** hota hai, jo do steps mein banta hai:\n1. **M ← C σ-bond:** CO ke carbon par maujood lone pair metal ke vacant hybrid orbital mein donate hota hai.\n2. **M → C π-bond (Back-bonding):** Metal ke **filled d-orbital** se ek electron pair CO ke **vacant antibonding π* orbital** mein wapas donate hota hai!\n\n```\nSynergic Bonding in M—C≡O\n\n        σ-donation (lone pair)\n      <------------------------\n    M                           C ≡ O\n      ------------------------>\n      π back-donation (filled d → vacant π* of CO)\n```\n\n**Nateeja (Result):**\n- Dono bonds ek-doosre ko mazboot banate hain (**synergic effect**)!\n- **M–C bond mazboot aur chhota** ho jata hai (bond order > 1).\n- **C–O bond thoda kamzor aur lamba** ho jata hai (kyunki electrons CO ke *antibonding* π* orbital mein gaye!)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "2. Stability of Coordination Compounds & Chelate Effect",
            "headingHinglish": "2. Stability of Coordination Compounds & Chelate Effect",
            "body": "Solution mein metal ion M aur n ligands L ke milne ka overall equilibrium:\nM + nL ⇌ MLₙ\n\n**Overall Stability Constant:** **βₙ = [MLₙ] / ([M][L]ⁿ) = K₁ × K₂ × K₃ × ... × Kₙ**\n- Jahan K₁, K₂, ..., Kₙ **stepwise stability constants** hain.\n- **Instability (dissociation) constant = 1 / βₙ.**\n- Jitni badi **log βₙ** value, utna zyada thermodynamically stable complex!\n\n### Factors Increasing Complex Stability:\n1. **Higher charge and smaller size of central metal ion** (high charge/radius ratio → stronger attraction for ligands; e.g., Fe³⁺ complexes are more stable than Fe²⁺ complexes).\n2. **Stronger Lewis base ligand** (jaise CN⁻, NH₃, en).\n3. **Chelate Effect:** Bidentate/polydentate ligands (en, ox, EDTA⁴⁻) 5- ya 6-membered rings banate hain jo monodentate complexes (jaise NH₃, H₂O) se kahin zyada stable hote hain (kyunki ring banne par solution mein zyada free solvent molecules release hote hain → **entropy ΔS badhti hai**!).",
            "bodyHinglish": "Solution mein metal ion M aur n ligands L ke milne ka overall equilibrium:\nM + nL ⇌ MLₙ\n\n**Overall Stability Constant:** **βₙ = [MLₙ] / ([M][L]ⁿ) = K₁ × K₂ × K₃ × ... × Kₙ**\n- Jahan K₁, K₂, ..., Kₙ **stepwise stability constants** hain.\n- **Instability (dissociation) constant = 1 / βₙ.**\n- Jitni badi **log βₙ** value, utna zyada thermodynamically stable complex!\n\n### Factors Increasing Complex Stability:\n1. **Higher charge and smaller size of central metal ion** (high charge/radius ratio → stronger attraction for ligands; e.g., Fe³⁺ complexes are more stable than Fe²⁺ complexes).\n2. **Stronger Lewis base ligand** (jaise CN⁻, NH₃, en).\n3. **Chelate Effect:** Bidentate/polydentate ligands (en, ox, EDTA⁴⁻) 5- ya 6-membered rings banate hain jo monodentate complexes (jaise NH₃, H₂O) se kahin zyada stable hote hain (kyunki ring banne par solution mein zyada free solvent molecules release hote hain → **entropy ΔS badhti hai**!)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "3. Importance and Applications of Coordination Compounds",
            "headingHinglish": "3. Importance and Applications of Coordination Compounds",
            "body": "1. **Biological Systems:**\n   - **Chlorophyll** (photosynthesis pigment): **Magnesium (Mg²⁺)** complex.\n   - **Haemoglobin** (oxygen carrier in blood): **Iron (Fe²⁺)** complex.\n   - **Vitamin B₁₂** (cyanocobalamin, anti-pernicious anaemia factor): **Cobalt (Co)** complex.\n   - **Carboxypeptidase A & Carbonic anhydrase** (enzymes): **Zinc (Zn²⁺)** complexes.\n2. **Medicinal Chemistry (Chelation Therapy & Anti-Cancer):**\n   - **Cisplatin** (cis-[Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂]): Effectively inhibits tumour growth in cancer chemotherapy.\n   - **Ca-EDTA (Calcium disodium EDTA):** **Lead (Pb²⁺) poisoning** ka ilaj (Pb²⁺ EDTA ke saath soluble complex banakar urine se bahar nikal jata hai!).\n   - **D-Penicillamine & Desferrioxamine B:** Body mein excess **Copper (Cu)** aur **Iron (Fe)** hatane ke liye (chelation therapy).\n3. **Analytical Chemistry & Water Hardness:**\n   - **EDTA titration:** Hard water mein **Ca²⁺ aur Mg²⁺** ki concentration napne ke liye (Ca²⁺ aur Mg²⁺ EDTA⁴⁻ ke saath stable 1:1 complexes banate hain).\n   - **Qualitative analysis:** **Dimethylglyoxime (DMG)** se Ni²⁺ ka rosy-red precipitate; **α-nitroso-β-naphthol** se Co²⁺; **cupron** se Cu²⁺.\n4. **Metallurgy (Extraction of Noble Metals — Industrial Process):**\n   - **Gold (Au) aur Silver (Ag)** ko ore se dilute NaCN/KCN + air se soluble complex **[Au(CN)₂]⁻** aur **[Ag(CN)₂]⁻** banakar extract kiya jata hai, phir Zn dust se precipitate karte hain!\n   - **Mond Process** (Ni purification): Impure Ni + 4CO (330–350 K) → volatile [Ni(CO)₄], jise 450–470 K par decompose karke pure Ni milta hai.\n5. **Black-and-White Photography:**\n   - Film par bacha hua unexposed **AgBr** **hypo solution (Na₂S₂O₃)** mein soluble complex **[Ag(S₂O₃)₂]³⁻** banakar dhul jata hai!\n   - AgBr + 2Na₂S₂O₃ → Na₃[Ag(S₂O₃)₂] + NaBr.\n6. **Industrial Catalysis:**\n   - **Wilkinson's catalyst** [(Ph₃P)₃RhCl]: Hydrogenation of alkenes.\n   - **Ziegler–Natta catalyst** (TiCl₄ + Al(C₂H₅)₃): Polymerisation of ethene to high-density polythene.\n7. **Electroplating:**\n   - Smooth, uniform silver/gold plating ke liye simple AgNO₃ ki jagah **[Ag(CN)₂]⁻** aur **[Au(CN)₂]⁻** baths use hote hain (slow, controlled release of metal ions).",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. **Biological Systems:**\n   - **Chlorophyll** (photosynthesis pigment): **Magnesium (Mg²⁺)** complex.\n   - **Haemoglobin** (oxygen carrier in blood): **Iron (Fe²⁺)** complex.\n   - **Vitamin B₁₂** (cyanocobalamin, anti-pernicious anaemia factor): **Cobalt (Co)** complex.\n   - **Carboxypeptidase A & Carbonic anhydrase** (enzymes): **Zinc (Zn²⁺)** complexes.\n2. **Medicinal Chemistry (Chelation Therapy & Anti-Cancer):**\n   - **Cisplatin** (cis-[Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂]): Effectively inhibits tumour growth in cancer chemotherapy.\n   - **Ca-EDTA (Calcium disodium EDTA):** **Lead (Pb²⁺) poisoning** ka ilaj (Pb²⁺ EDTA ke saath soluble complex banakar urine se bahar nikal jata hai!).\n   - **D-Penicillamine & Desferrioxamine B:** Body mein excess **Copper (Cu)** aur **Iron (Fe)** hatane ke liye (chelation therapy).\n3. **Analytical Chemistry & Water Hardness:**\n   - **EDTA titration:** Hard water mein **Ca²⁺ aur Mg²⁺** ki concentration napne ke liye (Ca²⁺ aur Mg²⁺ EDTA⁴⁻ ke saath stable 1:1 complexes banate hain).\n   - **Qualitative analysis:** **Dimethylglyoxime (DMG)** se Ni²⁺ ka rosy-red precipitate; **α-nitroso-β-naphthol** se Co²⁺; **cupron** se Cu²⁺.\n4. **Metallurgy (Extraction of Noble Metals — Industrial Process):**\n   - **Gold (Au) aur Silver (Ag)** ko ore se dilute NaCN/KCN + air se soluble complex **[Au(CN)₂]⁻** aur **[Ag(CN)₂]⁻** banakar extract kiya jata hai, phir Zn dust se precipitate karte hain!\n   - **Mond Process** (Ni purification): Impure Ni + 4CO (330–350 K) → volatile [Ni(CO)₄], jise 450–470 K par decompose karke pure Ni milta hai.\n5. **Black-and-White Photography:**\n   - Film par bacha hua unexposed **AgBr** **hypo solution (Na₂S₂O₃)** mein soluble complex **[Ag(S₂O₃)₂]³⁻** banakar dhul jata hai!\n   - AgBr + 2Na₂S₂O₃ → Na₃[Ag(S₂O₃)₂] + NaBr.\n6. **Industrial Catalysis:**\n   - **Wilkinson's catalyst** [(Ph₃P)₃RhCl]: Hydrogenation of alkenes.\n   - **Ziegler–Natta catalyst** (TiCl₄ + Al(C₂H₅)₃): Polymerisation of ethene to high-density polythene.\n7. **Electroplating:**\n   - Smooth, uniform silver/gold plating ke liye simple AgNO₃ ki jagah **[Ag(CN)₂]⁻** aur **[Au(CN)₂]⁻** baths use hote hain (slow, controlled release of metal ions)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Solved Examples",
            "headingHinglish": "Solved Examples",
            "body": "### Example 1: Calculating Overall Stability Constant β₄\nFor the formation of [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ in water, the four stepwise stability constants are:\nlog K₁ = 4.0, log K₂ = 3.2, log K₃ = 2.7, log K₄ = 2.0.\nCalculate log β₄ and β₄.\n\n**Solution:**\nSince β₄ = K₁ × K₂ × K₃ × K₄:\nlog β₄ = log K₁ + log K₂ + log K₃ + log K₄\n= 4.0 + 3.2 + 2.7 + 2.0 = **11.9**.\nTherefore, **β₄ = 10¹¹·⁹ = 7.94 × 10¹¹**.\n\nJaanch: 4.0 + 3.2 = 7.2; 7.2 + 2.7 = 9.9; 9.9 + 2.0 = 11.9 → very large β₄ means [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ is very stable ✅\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Explaining Chelate Stability\nWhy is [Ni(en)₃]²⁺ (log β = 18.6) far more stable than [Ni(NH₃)₆]²⁺ (log β = 8.6), even though both have six Ni–N bonds?\n\n**Solution:**\nIn [Ni(en)₃]²⁺, each bidentate ethane-1,2-diamine (en) ligand forms a stable **5-membered chelate ring** with Ni²⁺ (3 chelate rings total). When 3 en molecules displace 6 H₂O molecules from [Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺, the total number of free particles in solution increases from 4 to 7, producing a large **positive entropy change (ΔS > 0)** and more negative ΔG° = ΔH° − TΔS° (**chelate effect**).\n\nJaanch: [Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 3en → [Ni(en)₃]²⁺ + 6H₂O: 4 reactant particles → 7 product particles (ΔS > 0) ✅\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: Matching Real-Life Applications to Complexes\nMatch each application with the corresponding metal/complex:\n(a) Removal of excess lead from the human body\n(b) Fixing of photographic film\n(c) Hydrogenation of alkenes\n\n**Solution:**\n(a) **Ca-EDTA** (forms water-soluble [Pb(EDTA)]²⁻ excreted in urine)\n(b) **Sodium thiosulphate (Na₂S₂O₃)** forming soluble **[Ag(S₂O₃)₂]³⁻**\n(c) **Wilkinson's catalyst**, **[(Ph₃P)₃RhCl]**\n\nJaanch: Each application matches its exact industrial/medical complex ✅",
            "bodyHinglish": "### Example 1: Calculating Overall Stability Constant β₄\nFor the formation of [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ in water, the four stepwise stability constants are:\nlog K₁ = 4.0, log K₂ = 3.2, log K₃ = 2.7, log K₄ = 2.0.\nCalculate log β₄ and β₄.\n\n**Solution:**\nSince β₄ = K₁ × K₂ × K₃ × K₄:\nlog β₄ = log K₁ + log K₂ + log K₃ + log K₄\n= 4.0 + 3.2 + 2.7 + 2.0 = **11.9**.\nTherefore, **β₄ = 10¹¹·⁹ = 7.94 × 10¹¹**.\n\nJaanch: 4.0 + 3.2 = 7.2; 7.2 + 2.7 = 9.9; 9.9 + 2.0 = 11.9 → very large β₄ means [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ is very stable ✅\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Explaining Chelate Stability\nWhy is [Ni(en)₃]²⁺ (log β = 18.6) far more stable than [Ni(NH₃)₆]²⁺ (log β = 8.6), even though both have six Ni–N bonds?\n\n**Solution:**\nIn [Ni(en)₃]²⁺, each bidentate ethane-1,2-diamine (en) ligand forms a stable **5-membered chelate ring** with Ni²⁺ (3 chelate rings total). When 3 en molecules displace 6 H₂O molecules from [Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺, the total number of free particles in solution increases from 4 to 7, producing a large **positive entropy change (ΔS > 0)** and more negative ΔG° = ΔH° − TΔS° (**chelate effect**).\n\nJaanch: [Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 3en → [Ni(en)₃]²⁺ + 6H₂O: 4 reactant particles → 7 product particles (ΔS > 0) ✅\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: Matching Real-Life Applications to Complexes\nMatch each application with the corresponding metal/complex:\n(a) Removal of excess lead from the human body\n(b) Fixing of photographic film\n(c) Hydrogenation of alkenes\n\n**Solution:**\n(a) **Ca-EDTA** (forms water-soluble [Pb(EDTA)]²⁻ excreted in urine)\n(b) **Sodium thiosulphate (Na₂S₂O₃)** forming soluble **[Ag(S₂O₃)₂]³⁻**\n(c) **Wilkinson's catalyst**, **[(Ph₃P)₃RhCl]**\n\nJaanch: Each application matches its exact industrial/medical complex ✅"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Common Exam Mistakes (Watch Out!)",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam Galtiyan (Common Mistakes)",
            "body": "1. **Synergic back-bonding se C–O bond ko mazboot bata dena:**\n   - *Galat:* Back-bonding se M–C aur C–O dono bonds mazboot ho jate hain.\n   - *Sahi:* Back-bonding CO ke **antibonding π*** orbital mein electrons bhejti hai — isliye **M–C bond mazboot** hota hai, par **C–O bond kamzor (lamba)** ho jata hai!\n\n2. **Chlorophyll aur Haemoglobin ke metals mix kar dena:**\n   - *Galat:* Chlorophyll mein Iron hota hai.\n   - *Sahi:* **Chlorophyll = Mg²⁺**, **Haemoglobin = Fe²⁺**, **Vitamin B₁₂ = Co**!\n\n3. **Stepwise K values ko add karke β nikal lena:**\n   - *Galat:* β₄ = K₁ + K₂ + K₃ + K₄.\n   - *Sahi:* **β₄ = K₁ × K₂ × K₃ × K₄** (multiply hote hain!); **log β₄** mein log K values **add** hoti hain!",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. **Synergic back-bonding se C–O bond ko mazboot bata dena:**\n   - *Galat:* Back-bonding se M–C aur C–O dono bonds mazboot ho jate hain.\n   - *Sahi:* Back-bonding CO ke **antibonding π*** orbital mein electrons bhejti hai — isliye **M–C bond mazboot** hota hai, par **C–O bond kamzor (lamba)** ho jata hai!\n\n2. **Chlorophyll aur Haemoglobin ke metals mix kar dena:**\n   - *Galat:* Chlorophyll mein Iron hota hai.\n   - *Sahi:* **Chlorophyll = Mg²⁺**, **Haemoglobin = Fe²⁺**, **Vitamin B₁₂ = Co**!\n\n3. **Stepwise K values ko add karke β nikal lena:**\n   - *Galat:* β₄ = K₁ + K₂ + K₃ + K₄.\n   - *Sahi:* **β₄ = K₁ × K₂ × K₃ × K₄** (multiply hote hain!); **log β₄** mein log K values **add** hoti hain!"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- **Metal Carbonyls:** Homoleptic CO complexes ([Ni(CO)₄] tetrahedral, [Fe(CO)₅] TBP, [Cr(CO)₆] octahedral, [Mn₂(CO)₁₀] has Mn–Mn bond, [Co₂(CO)₈] has Co–Co + 2 bridging CO).\n- **Synergic bonding:** C → M σ-bond + M → C π* back-bond (strengthens M–C, weakens C–O).\n- **Stability:** βₙ = K₁ × K₂ × ... × Kₙ; **Chelate effect** gives extra stability via positive ΔS.\n- **Applications:** Chlorophyll (Mg), Haemoglobin (Fe), Vitamin B₁₂ (Co), Cisplatin (cancer), EDTA (hard water Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺ & Pb poisoning), [Ag(S₂O₃)₂]³⁻ (photography), [(Ph₃P)₃RhCl] (Wilkinson's catalyst).",
            "bodyHinglish": "- **Metal Carbonyls:** Homoleptic CO complexes ([Ni(CO)₄] tetrahedral, [Fe(CO)₅] TBP, [Cr(CO)₆] octahedral, [Mn₂(CO)₁₀] has Mn–Mn bond, [Co₂(CO)₈] has Co–Co + 2 bridging CO).\n- **Synergic bonding:** C → M σ-bond + M → C π* back-bond (strengthens M–C, weakens C–O).\n- **Stability:** βₙ = K₁ × K₂ × ... × Kₙ; **Chelate effect** gives extra stability via positive ΔS.\n- **Applications:** Chlorophyll (Mg), Haemoglobin (Fe), Vitamin B₁₂ (Co), Cisplatin (cancer), EDTA (hard water Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺ & Pb poisoning), [Ag(S₂O₃)₂]³⁻ (photography), [(Ph₃P)₃RhCl] (Wilkinson's catalyst)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. Explain synergic bonding in metal carbonyls with a labelled diagram.\n2. What effect does π back-bonding have on the bond lengths of M–C and C–O bonds in metal carbonyls?\n3. Describe the structures of [Fe(CO)₅], [Cr(CO)₆], and [Mn₂(CO)₁₀].\n4. Define overall stability constant (β₄) and write its relationship with stepwise stability constants K₁, K₂, K₃, and K₄.\n5. What is the chelate effect? Give one example comparing a chelate complex with a non-chelate complex.\n6. Name the central metal ion present in: (a) Chlorophyll, (b) Haemoglobin, (c) Vitamin B₁₂.\n7. How is EDTA used in: (a) estimating hardness of water, (b) treating lead poisoning?\n8. Write the chemical reaction involved in fixing photographic film using hypo (Na₂S₂O₃) solution.\n9. What is Wilkinson's catalyst? Write its formula and one major application.\n10. Why is the dissociation (instability) constant of a very stable complex extremely small? If β₄ = 2.0 × 10¹², find the instability constant.\n\n---\n\n### Answers\n\n1. In metal carbonyls, CO donates a lone pair from carbon to a vacant metal orbital (M ← C **σ-bond**), and the metal donates a pair of electrons from a filled d-orbital back into the vacant **π* antibonding orbital** of CO (M → C **π-bond**). Both mutually reinforce each other (**synergic bonding**).\n2. Back-bonding increases M–C bond order (making **M–C shorter and stronger**) while populating CO's antibonding π* orbital (making **C–O longer and weaker**).\n3. **[Fe(CO)₅]:** Trigonal bipyramidal; **[Cr(CO)₆]:** Octahedral; **[Mn₂(CO)₁₀]:** Two square-pyramidal Mn(CO)₅ units linked by a direct Mn–Mn single bond (no bridging CO).\n4. Overall stability constant β₄ is the equilibrium constant for M + 4L ⇌ ML₄. Relation: **β₄ = K₁ × K₂ × K₃ × K₄** (and log β₄ = log K₁ + log K₂ + log K₃ + log K₄).\n5. The extra thermodynamic stability of complexes formed by polydentate (chelate) ligands compared to analogous monodentate ligands (largely driven by entropy increase). Example: **[Ni(en)₃]²⁺** is much more stable than **[Ni(NH₃)₆]²⁺**.\n6. (a) **Mg²⁺ (Magnesium)**, (b) **Fe²⁺ (Iron)**, (c) **Co (Cobalt)**.\n7. (a) **Water hardness:** Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions are titrated against Na₂EDTA because they form stable 1:1 complexes with EDTA⁴⁻. (b) **Lead poisoning:** Ca-EDTA exchanges Ca²⁺ for Pb²⁺ in the body, forming soluble [Pb(EDTA)]²⁻ which is safely excreted in urine.\n8. **AgBr + 2Na₂S₂O₃ → Na₃[Ag(S₂O₃)₂] + NaBr** (soluble complex washes away unexposed AgBr).\n9. Wilkinson's catalyst is **[(Ph₃P)₃RhCl]** (chloridotris(triphenylphosphine)rhodium(I)); used for the **selective catalytic hydrogenation of alkenes**.\n10. Instability constant = 1 / βₙ. When βₙ is very large, 1/βₙ is tiny.\n    Instability constant = 1 / (2.0 × 10¹²) = **5.0 × 10⁻¹³**.\n    Jaanch: (2.0 × 10¹²) × (5.0 × 10⁻¹³) = 10 × 10⁻¹ = 1 ✅\n\n---\n\nAb tumne metal carbonyls, synergic bonding, chelate effect aur real-world coordination chemistry poori tarah samajh li hai!\n\n**Agle topic mein:** [Sab Kuch Mila Kar: Mixed Problems & Chapter Wrap-up](coordination-compounds-part5.md) mein hum poore Coordination Compounds chapter ke mixed high-scoring problems solve karenge aur summary table banayenge!",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. Explain synergic bonding in metal carbonyls with a labelled diagram.\n2. What effect does π back-bonding have on the bond lengths of M–C and C–O bonds in metal carbonyls?\n3. Describe the structures of [Fe(CO)₅], [Cr(CO)₆], and [Mn₂(CO)₁₀].\n4. Define overall stability constant (β₄) and write its relationship with stepwise stability constants K₁, K₂, K₃, and K₄.\n5. What is the chelate effect? Give one example comparing a chelate complex with a non-chelate complex.\n6. Name the central metal ion present in: (a) Chlorophyll, (b) Haemoglobin, (c) Vitamin B₁₂.\n7. How is EDTA used in: (a) estimating hardness of water, (b) treating lead poisoning?\n8. Write the chemical reaction involved in fixing photographic film using hypo (Na₂S₂O₃) solution.\n9. What is Wilkinson's catalyst? Write its formula and one major application.\n10. Why is the dissociation (instability) constant of a very stable complex extremely small? If β₄ = 2.0 × 10¹², find the instability constant.\n\n---\n\n### Answers\n\n1. In metal carbonyls, CO donates a lone pair from carbon to a vacant metal orbital (M ← C **σ-bond**), and the metal donates a pair of electrons from a filled d-orbital back into the vacant **π* antibonding orbital** of CO (M → C **π-bond**). Both mutually reinforce each other (**synergic bonding**).\n2. Back-bonding increases M–C bond order (making **M–C shorter and stronger**) while populating CO's antibonding π* orbital (making **C–O longer and weaker**).\n3. **[Fe(CO)₅]:** Trigonal bipyramidal; **[Cr(CO)₆]:** Octahedral; **[Mn₂(CO)₁₀]:** Two square-pyramidal Mn(CO)₅ units linked by a direct Mn–Mn single bond (no bridging CO).\n4. Overall stability constant β₄ is the equilibrium constant for M + 4L ⇌ ML₄. Relation: **β₄ = K₁ × K₂ × K₃ × K₄** (and log β₄ = log K₁ + log K₂ + log K₃ + log K₄).\n5. The extra thermodynamic stability of complexes formed by polydentate (chelate) ligands compared to analogous monodentate ligands (largely driven by entropy increase). Example: **[Ni(en)₃]²⁺** is much more stable than **[Ni(NH₃)₆]²⁺**.\n6. (a) **Mg²⁺ (Magnesium)**, (b) **Fe²⁺ (Iron)**, (c) **Co (Cobalt)**.\n7. (a) **Water hardness:** Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions are titrated against Na₂EDTA because they form stable 1:1 complexes with EDTA⁴⁻. (b) **Lead poisoning:** Ca-EDTA exchanges Ca²⁺ for Pb²⁺ in the body, forming soluble [Pb(EDTA)]²⁻ which is safely excreted in urine.\n8. **AgBr + 2Na₂S₂O₃ → Na₃[Ag(S₂O₃)₂] + NaBr** (soluble complex washes away unexposed AgBr).\n9. Wilkinson's catalyst is **[(Ph₃P)₃RhCl]** (chloridotris(triphenylphosphine)rhodium(I)); used for the **selective catalytic hydrogenation of alkenes**.\n10. Instability constant = 1 / βₙ. When βₙ is very large, 1/βₙ is tiny.\n    Instability constant = 1 / (2.0 × 10¹²) = **5.0 × 10⁻¹³**.\n    Jaanch: (2.0 × 10¹²) × (5.0 × 10⁻¹³) = 10 × 10⁻¹ = 1 ✅\n\n---\n\nAb tumne metal carbonyls, synergic bonding, chelate effect aur real-world coordination chemistry poori tarah samajh li hai!\n\n**Agle topic mein:** [Sab Kuch Mila Kar: Mixed Problems & Chapter Wrap-up](coordination-compounds-part5.md) mein hum poore Coordination Compounds chapter ke mixed high-scoring problems solve karenge aur summary table banayenge!"
          }
