          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. Write the IUPAC name, oxidation state, and coordination number of K₃[Fe(CN)₆].\n2. Predict the hybridisation, geometry, and spin-only magnetic moment of [MnBr₄]²⁻ (Mn: Z = 25; Br⁻ is weak-field).\n3. Which of the following complexes shows BOTH geometrical and optical isomerism: [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺, [Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]⁺, [Co(en)₂Cl₂]⁺, [Co(en)₃]³⁺?\n4. Why is [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ an inner-orbital complex whereas [Ni(NH₃)₆]²⁺ is an outer-orbital complex?\n5. Give the chemical formula and IUPAC name of the linkage isomer of [Co(NH₃)₅(SCN)]Cl₂.\n6. Arrange [CoF₆]³⁻, [Co(H₂O)₆]³⁺, and [Co(CN)₆]³⁻ in increasing order of crystal field splitting energy Δₒ.\n7. Explain why [Cr(NH₃)₆]³⁺ is paramagnetic while [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ is diamagnetic.\n8. A complex Co(NH₃)₅BrSO₄ exists in two isomeric forms A and B. Isomer A gives a white precipitate with BaCl₂, while Isomer B gives a pale yellow precipitate with AgNO₃. Write the formulas and IUPAC names of A and B.\n9. Why does the C–O bond length in [Fe(CO)₅] increase slightly compared to free CO gas?\n10. Name the coordination compound or metal complex used in: (a) cancer chemotherapy, (b) removal of hardness of water, (c) extraction of gold from its ore.\n\n---\n\n### Answers\n\n1. IUPAC name: **potassium hexacyanidoferrate(III)**. Oxidation state of Fe = **+3**; **CN = 6**.\n2. Mn²⁺ = [Ar] 3d⁵. With weak-field Br⁻ (CN = 4), hybridisation is **sp³ (tetrahedral)** with **5 unpaired electrons**.\n   μ = √[5(5 + 2)] = √35 = **5.92 BM**.\n   Jaanch: Tetrahedral always high-spin → n = 5 → √35 = 5.92 BM ✅\n3. **[Co(en)₂Cl₂]⁺** shows BOTH geometrical (cis and trans) and optical (d-cis and l-cis) isomerism!\n4. Co³⁺ = 3d⁶: with strong NH₃, all 6 electrons pair in three 3d orbitals, leaving two empty 3d orbitals for **d²sp³ (inner orbital)**. Ni²⁺ = 3d⁸: even after pairing, 8 electrons occupy at least four 3d orbitals, leaving only ONE 3d orbital free — not enough for d²sp³! So Ni²⁺ must use outer 4d orbitals (**sp³d², outer orbital**).\n5. Linkage isomer: **[Co(NH₃)₅(NCS)]Cl₂**.\n   IUPAC name: **pentaammineisothiocyanato-N-cobalt(III) chloride** (or pentaamminethiocyanato-N-cobalt(III) chloride).\n6. By spectrochemical series (F⁻ < H₂O < CN⁻):\n   **[CoF₆]³⁻ < [Co(H₂O)₆]³⁺ < [Co(CN)₆]³⁻**.\n7. [Cr(NH₃)₆]³⁺ has Cr³⁺ (3d³, t₂g³ e_g⁰) with **3 unpaired electrons** (μ = 3.87 BM, paramagnetic). [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ has Ni²⁺ (3d⁸) paired by strong CN⁻ into four 3d orbitals (dsp², **0 unpaired electrons**, diamagnetic).\n8. - **Isomer A** (gives BaSO₄ ppt → SO₄²⁻ outside): **[Co(NH₃)₅Br]SO₄** — pentaamminebromidocobalt(III) sulphate.\n   - **Isomer B** (gives AgBr ppt → Br⁻ outside): **[Co(NH₃)₅(SO₄)]Br** — pentaamminesulphatocobalt(III) bromide.\n9. Due to **synergic π back-bonding** from filled Fe d-orbitals into the **vacant antibonding π* orbitals** of CO, the C–O bond order decreases slightly, making the C–O bond longer than in free CO.\n10. (a) **Cisplatin** (cis-[Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂]), (b) **EDTA** (Na₂EDTA), (c) **Cyanide complex [Au(CN)₂]⁻** (using NaCN/KCN + air).",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. Write the IUPAC name, oxidation state, and coordination number of K₃[Fe(CN)₆].\n2. Predict the hybridisation, geometry, and spin-only magnetic moment of [MnBr₄]²⁻ (Mn: Z = 25; Br⁻ is weak-field).\n3. Which of the following complexes shows BOTH geometrical and optical isomerism: [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺, [Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]⁺, [Co(en)₂Cl₂]⁺, [Co(en)₃]³⁺?\n4. Why is [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ an inner-orbital complex whereas [Ni(NH₃)₆]²⁺ is an outer-orbital complex?\n5. Give the chemical formula and IUPAC name of the linkage isomer of [Co(NH₃)₅(SCN)]Cl₂.\n6. Arrange [CoF₆]³⁻, [Co(H₂O)₆]³⁺, and [Co(CN)₆]³⁻ in increasing order of crystal field splitting energy Δₒ.\n7. Explain why [Cr(NH₃)₆]³⁺ is paramagnetic while [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ is diamagnetic.\n8. A complex Co(NH₃)₅BrSO₄ exists in two isomeric forms A and B. Isomer A gives a white precipitate with BaCl₂, while Isomer B gives a pale yellow precipitate with AgNO₃. Write the formulas and IUPAC names of A and B.\n9. Why does the C–O bond length in [Fe(CO)₅] increase slightly compared to free CO gas?\n10. Name the coordination compound or metal complex used in: (a) cancer chemotherapy, (b) removal of hardness of water, (c) extraction of gold from its ore.\n\n---\n\n### Answers\n\n1. IUPAC name: **potassium hexacyanidoferrate(III)**. Oxidation state of Fe = **+3**; **CN = 6**.\n2. Mn²⁺ = [Ar] 3d⁵. With weak-field Br⁻ (CN = 4), hybridisation is **sp³ (tetrahedral)** with **5 unpaired electrons**.\n   μ = √[5(5 + 2)] = √35 = **5.92 BM**.\n   Jaanch: Tetrahedral always high-spin → n = 5 → √35 = 5.92 BM ✅\n3. **[Co(en)₂Cl₂]⁺** shows BOTH geometrical (cis and trans) and optical (d-cis and l-cis) isomerism!\n4. Co³⁺ = 3d⁶: with strong NH₃, all 6 electrons pair in three 3d orbitals, leaving two empty 3d orbitals for **d²sp³ (inner orbital)**. Ni²⁺ = 3d⁸: even after pairing, 8 electrons occupy at least four 3d orbitals, leaving only ONE 3d orbital free — not enough for d²sp³! So Ni²⁺ must use outer 4d orbitals (**sp³d², outer orbital**).\n5. Linkage isomer: **[Co(NH₃)₅(NCS)]Cl₂**.\n   IUPAC name: **pentaammineisothiocyanato-N-cobalt(III) chloride** (or pentaamminethiocyanato-N-cobalt(III) chloride).\n6. By spectrochemical series (F⁻ < H₂O < CN⁻):\n   **[CoF₆]³⁻ < [Co(H₂O)₆]³⁺ < [Co(CN)₆]³⁻**.\n7. [Cr(NH₃)₆]³⁺ has Cr³⁺ (3d³, t₂g³ e_g⁰) with **3 unpaired electrons** (μ = 3.87 BM, paramagnetic). [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ has Ni²⁺ (3d⁸) paired by strong CN⁻ into four 3d orbitals (dsp², **0 unpaired electrons**, diamagnetic).\n8. - **Isomer A** (gives BaSO₄ ppt → SO₄²⁻ outside): **[Co(NH₃)₅Br]SO₄** — pentaamminebromidocobalt(III) sulphate.\n   - **Isomer B** (gives AgBr ppt → Br⁻ outside): **[Co(NH₃)₅(SO₄)]Br** — pentaamminesulphatocobalt(III) bromide.\n9. Due to **synergic π back-bonding** from filled Fe d-orbitals into the **vacant antibonding π* orbitals** of CO, the C–O bond order decreases slightly, making the C–O bond longer than in free CO.\n10. (a) **Cisplatin** (cis-[Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂]), (b) **EDTA** (Na₂EDTA), (c) **Cyanide complex [Au(CN)₂]⁻** (using NaCN/KCN + air)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Chapter Summary & Wrap-Up",
            "headingHinglish": "Chapter Ka Saar (Coordination Compounds Wrap-up)",
            "body": "| Topic | Key Concept / Formula | Must-Remember Fact |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Werner & IUPAC | Primary = OS; Secondary = CN | Alphabetical ligands → metal (−ate if anionic) → Roman OS; en/ox = bidentate, EDTA = hexadentate |\n| 2. Isomerism | Structural (4) + Geometrical + Optical | No cis/trans in tetrahedral; Ma₃b₃ → fac/mer; [M(AA)₃] & cis-[M(AA)₂b₂] optically active |\n| 3. VBT & CFT | Δₒ (t₂g −0.4, e_g +0.6); Δₜ = (4/9) Δₒ | Strong ligand (CN⁻, CO) → low spin; Weak ligand (F⁻, Cl⁻) → high spin; [Ni(CO)₄] = sp³ (μ=0) |\n| 4. Carbonyls & Stability | Synergic σ + π*; βₙ = K₁×K₂×...×Kₙ | Back-bonding strengthens M–C, weakens C–O; Chelate effect boosts stability (ΔS > 0) |\n| 5. Applications | Bio, medicine, metallurgy, photography | Chlorophyll (Mg), Haemoglobin (Fe), B₁₂ (Co), Cisplatin, EDTA, [Ag(S₂O₃)₂]³⁻, Wilkinson's catalyst |\n\n---\n\nAb tumne Coordination Compounds ke saare core topics master kar liye hain! Competitive exams (JEE/NEET) ke liye **CFSE calculation, Effective Atomic Number (EAN) rule, Jahn–Teller distortion, aur complex stereoisomer counting** padhne ke liye [Coordination Compounds — Gehrai Mein (Advanced Level)](coordination-compounds-advanced.md) zaroor dekho.\n\n**Agle chapter mein:** Hum Organic Chemistry ki duniya mein enter karenge — **Haloalkanes and Haloarenes** — jahan SN1, SN2, chirality aur organic synthesis ke reactions shuru honge!",
            "bodyHinglish": "| Topic | Key Concept / Formula | Must-Remember Fact |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Werner & IUPAC | Primary = OS; Secondary = CN | Alphabetical ligands → metal (−ate if anionic) → Roman OS; en/ox = bidentate, EDTA = hexadentate |\n| 2. Isomerism | Structural (4) + Geometrical + Optical | No cis/trans in tetrahedral; Ma₃b₃ → fac/mer; [M(AA)₃] & cis-[M(AA)₂b₂] optically active |\n| 3. VBT & CFT | Δₒ (t₂g −0.4, e_g +0.6); Δₜ = (4/9) Δₒ | Strong ligand (CN⁻, CO) → low spin; Weak ligand (F⁻, Cl⁻) → high spin; [Ni(CO)₄] = sp³ (μ=0) |\n| 4. Carbonyls & Stability | Synergic σ + π*; βₙ = K₁×K₂×...×Kₙ | Back-bonding strengthens M–C, weakens C–O; Chelate effect boosts stability (ΔS > 0) |\n| 5. Applications | Bio, medicine, metallurgy, photography | Chlorophyll (Mg), Haemoglobin (Fe), B₁₂ (Co), Cisplatin, EDTA, [Ag(S₂O₃)₂]³⁻, Wilkinson's catalyst |\n\n---\n\nAb tumne Coordination Compounds ke saare core topics master kar liye hain! Competitive exams (JEE/NEET) ke liye **CFSE calculation, Effective Atomic Number (EAN) rule, Jahn–Teller distortion, aur complex stereoisomer counting** padhne ke liye [Coordination Compounds — Gehrai Mein (Advanced Level)](coordination-compounds-advanced.md) zaroor dekho.\n\n**Agle chapter mein:** Hum Organic Chemistry ki duniya mein enter karenge — **Haloalkanes and Haloarenes** — jahan SN1, SN2, chirality aur organic synthesis ke reactions shuru honge!"
          }
