          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. Distinguish between allylic halides and vinylic halides with one example each.\n2. What is the difference between a gem-dihalide and a vic-dihalide? Give the IUPAC name of ethylidene chloride and ethylene dichloride.\n3. Classify the following as 1°, 2°, 3°, allylic, benzylic, or aryl halides:\n   (a) (CH₃)₃C–Cl, (b) C₆H₅–Br, (c) CH₂=CH–CH(Cl)–CH₃, (d) C₆H₅–CH₂–I.\n4. Write the IUPAC names of:\n   (a) CH₃–CH₂–CH(Br)–CH₂–CH₃\n   (b) CH₂=CH–CH₂–Cl\n   (c) (CH₃)₃C–CH₂–Br\n5. Write the structures of:\n   (a) 2-Chloro-3-methylpentane\n   (b) 1,4-Dibromobut-2-ene\n   (c) 1-Bromo-4-sec-butyl-2-methylbenzene\n6. Why is the C–X bond in haloalkanes polar in nature?\n7. Arrange C–F, C–Cl, C–Br, and C–I bonds in increasing order of bond enthalpy. Which alkyl halide reacts fastest when C–X bond breaks?\n8. Explain why chloromethane (CH₃Cl) has a higher dipole moment than fluoromethane (CH₃F).\n9. Why is chlorobenzene's C–Cl bond shorter (169 pm) than that of chloromethane (178 pm)?\n10. Write all structural isomers of molecular formula C₄H₉Br and give their IUPAC names.\n\n---\n\n### Answers\n\n1. In **allylic halides**, halogen is bonded to an **sp³ carbon** adjacent to a C=C double bond (e.g., CH₂=CH–CH₂–Cl). In **vinylic halides**, halogen is directly bonded to an **sp² carbon** of the C=C double bond (e.g., CH₂=CH–Cl).\n2. **gem-Dihalide** has both halogens on the **same carbon**; **vic-dihalide** has halogens on **adjacent carbons**.\n   - Ethylidene chloride (CH₃–CHCl₂) = **1,1-Dichloroethane**.\n   - Ethylene dichloride (Cl–CH₂–CH₂–Cl) = **1,2-Dichloroethane**.\n3. (a) **3° alkyl halide**, (b) **aryl halide**, (c) **2° allylic halide**, (d) **1° benzylic halide**.\n4. (a) **3-Bromopentane**\n   (b) **3-Chloroprop-1-ene**\n   (c) **1-Bromo-2,2-dimethylpropane**\n5. (a) CH₃–CH(Cl)–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₃\n   (b) Br–CH₂–CH=CH–CH₂–Br\n   (c) Benzene ring with –Br at C1, –CH₃ at C2, and –CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃ at C4.\n6. Because halogens are more electronegative than carbon, they pull the shared bonding electron pair toward themselves, creating partial negative charge (δ⁻) on X and partial positive charge (δ⁺) on C.\n7. Increasing bond enthalpy: **C–I < C–Br < C–Cl < C–F**. **Alkyl iodides (R–I)** react fastest because C–I has the lowest bond enthalpy (weakest bond).\n8. Because dipole moment μ = q × d, and the C–Cl bond (178 pm) is significantly longer than C–F (139 pm), so the larger distance factor in CH₃Cl outweighs fluorine's higher electronegativity.\n9. Two reasons: (i) Carbon in chlorobenzene is **sp² hybridised** (higher s-character = shorter bond) vs sp³ in CH₃Cl; (ii) **resonance** delocalises Cl's lone pair into the benzene ring, giving the C–Cl bond partial double-bond character.\n10. Four structural isomers of C₄H₉Br:\n    1. CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–Br: **1-Bromobutane** (1°)\n    2. CH₃–CH₂–CH(Br)–CH₃: **2-Bromobutane** (2°)\n    3. (CH₃)₂CH–CH₂–Br: **1-Bromo-2-methylpropane** (1°)\n    4. (CH₃)₃C–Br: **2-Bromo-2-methylpropane** (3°)\n    Jaanch: All 4 isomers have formula C₄H₉Br (4 carbons, 9 hydrogens, 1 bromine) ✅\n\n---\n\nAb tum kisi bhi haloalkane ya haloarene ko classify aur name kar sakte ho aur C–X bond ki polarity samajh chuke ho.\n\n**Agle topic mein:** Hum [Methods of Preparation of Haloalkanes and Haloarenes](haloalkanes-and-haloarenes-part2.md) padhenge — jahan **Darzen's method (SOCl₂), Finkelstein, Swarts aur Sandmeyer** jaise classic named reactions aayenge!",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. Distinguish between allylic halides and vinylic halides with one example each.\n2. What is the difference between a gem-dihalide and a vic-dihalide? Give the IUPAC name of ethylidene chloride and ethylene dichloride.\n3. Classify the following as 1°, 2°, 3°, allylic, benzylic, or aryl halides:\n   (a) (CH₃)₃C–Cl, (b) C₆H₅–Br, (c) CH₂=CH–CH(Cl)–CH₃, (d) C₆H₅–CH₂–I.\n4. Write the IUPAC names of:\n   (a) CH₃–CH₂–CH(Br)–CH₂–CH₃\n   (b) CH₂=CH–CH₂–Cl\n   (c) (CH₃)₃C–CH₂–Br\n5. Write the structures of:\n   (a) 2-Chloro-3-methylpentane\n   (b) 1,4-Dibromobut-2-ene\n   (c) 1-Bromo-4-sec-butyl-2-methylbenzene\n6. Why is the C–X bond in haloalkanes polar in nature?\n7. Arrange C–F, C–Cl, C–Br, and C–I bonds in increasing order of bond enthalpy. Which alkyl halide reacts fastest when C–X bond breaks?\n8. Explain why chloromethane (CH₃Cl) has a higher dipole moment than fluoromethane (CH₃F).\n9. Why is chlorobenzene's C–Cl bond shorter (169 pm) than that of chloromethane (178 pm)?\n10. Write all structural isomers of molecular formula C₄H₉Br and give their IUPAC names.\n\n---\n\n### Answers\n\n1. In **allylic halides**, halogen is bonded to an **sp³ carbon** adjacent to a C=C double bond (e.g., CH₂=CH–CH₂–Cl). In **vinylic halides**, halogen is directly bonded to an **sp² carbon** of the C=C double bond (e.g., CH₂=CH–Cl).\n2. **gem-Dihalide** has both halogens on the **same carbon**; **vic-dihalide** has halogens on **adjacent carbons**.\n   - Ethylidene chloride (CH₃–CHCl₂) = **1,1-Dichloroethane**.\n   - Ethylene dichloride (Cl–CH₂–CH₂–Cl) = **1,2-Dichloroethane**.\n3. (a) **3° alkyl halide**, (b) **aryl halide**, (c) **2° allylic halide**, (d) **1° benzylic halide**.\n4. (a) **3-Bromopentane**\n   (b) **3-Chloroprop-1-ene**\n   (c) **1-Bromo-2,2-dimethylpropane**\n5. (a) CH₃–CH(Cl)–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₃\n   (b) Br–CH₂–CH=CH–CH₂–Br\n   (c) Benzene ring with –Br at C1, –CH₃ at C2, and –CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃ at C4.\n6. Because halogens are more electronegative than carbon, they pull the shared bonding electron pair toward themselves, creating partial negative charge (δ⁻) on X and partial positive charge (δ⁺) on C.\n7. Increasing bond enthalpy: **C–I < C–Br < C–Cl < C–F**. **Alkyl iodides (R–I)** react fastest because C–I has the lowest bond enthalpy (weakest bond).\n8. Because dipole moment μ = q × d, and the C–Cl bond (178 pm) is significantly longer than C–F (139 pm), so the larger distance factor in CH₃Cl outweighs fluorine's higher electronegativity.\n9. Two reasons: (i) Carbon in chlorobenzene is **sp² hybridised** (higher s-character = shorter bond) vs sp³ in CH₃Cl; (ii) **resonance** delocalises Cl's lone pair into the benzene ring, giving the C–Cl bond partial double-bond character.\n10. Four structural isomers of C₄H₉Br:\n    1. CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–Br: **1-Bromobutane** (1°)\n    2. CH₃–CH₂–CH(Br)–CH₃: **2-Bromobutane** (2°)\n    3. (CH₃)₂CH–CH₂–Br: **1-Bromo-2-methylpropane** (1°)\n    4. (CH₃)₃C–Br: **2-Bromo-2-methylpropane** (3°)\n    Jaanch: All 4 isomers have formula C₄H₉Br (4 carbons, 9 hydrogens, 1 bromine) ✅\n\n---\n\nAb tum kisi bhi haloalkane ya haloarene ko classify aur name kar sakte ho aur C–X bond ki polarity samajh chuke ho.\n\n**Agle topic mein:** Hum [Methods of Preparation of Haloalkanes and Haloarenes](haloalkanes-and-haloarenes-part2.md) padhenge — jahan **Darzen's method (SOCl₂), Finkelstein, Swarts aur Sandmeyer** jaise classic named reactions aayenge!"
          }
