            "heading": "1. Preparation of Alcohols",
            "headingHinglish": "1. Preparation of Alcohols",
            "body": "### (a) From Alkenes (2 Complementary Methods!)\n1. **Acid-Catalysed Hydration (Markovnikov Addition):**\n   CH₃–CH=CH₂ + H₂O —(H⁺)→ **CH₃–CH(OH)–CH₃ (Propan-2-ol)**\n   - **3-Step Mechanism (Exam Favourite!):**\n     - Step 1: Protonation of alkene to form more stable **carbocation** (H⁺ + H₂O ⇌ H₃O⁺; CH₃–CH=CH₂ + H₃O⁺ → CH₃–CH⁺–CH₃ + H₂O).\n     - Step 2: Nucleophilic attack of H₂O on carbocation → protonated alcohol.\n     - Step 3: Deprotonation (loss of H⁺) → alcohol + H₃O⁺ regenerated.\n2. **Hydroboration–Oxidation (Anti-Markovnikov Addition):**\n   3CH₃–CH=CH₂ + ½(BH₃)₂ → (CH₃–CH₂–CH₂)₃B —(H₂O₂ / aq. NaOH)→ **3CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–OH (Propan-1-ol)** + B(OH)₃\n   - Result: –OH goes to the **less substituted (terminal)** carbon!\n\n### (b) By Reduction of Carbonyl Compounds & Carboxylic Acids\n- **Aldehyde → 1° alcohol:** R–CHO + H₂ (Pd/Ni) ya NaBH₄ ya LiAlH₄ → **R–CH₂OH**.\n- **Ketone → 2° alcohol:** R–CO–R' + NaBH₄ ya LiAlH₄ → **R–CH(OH)–R'**.\n- **Carboxylic acid / Ester → 1° alcohol:** Needs strong reducing agent **LiAlH₄** (NaBH₄ acids/esters ko reduce nahi karta!). Industrially, R–COOH → ester → catalytic hydrogenation (H₂ / catalyst) → R–CH₂OH (cheaper than LiAlH₄!).\n\n### (c) From Grignard Reagents (R–Mg–X + Carbonyl → Alcohol!) — SUPER IMPORTANT!\nStep 1: Nucleophilic addition in dry ether; Step 2: Acidic hydrolysis (H₂O / H⁺):\n1. **Methanal (HCHO) + R–Mg–X → 1° Alcohol** (R–CH₂OH)\n2. **Any other Aldehyde (R'–CHO) + R–Mg–X → 2° Alcohol** (R'–CH(OH)–R)\n3. **Any Ketone (R'–CO–R'') + R–Mg–X → 3° Alcohol** (R'R''C(OH)–R)",
            "bodyHinglish": "### (a) From Alkenes (2 Complementary Methods!)\n1. **Acid-Catalysed Hydration (Markovnikov Addition):**\n   CH₃–CH=CH₂ + H₂O —(H⁺)→ **CH₃–CH(OH)–CH₃ (Propan-2-ol)**\n   - **3-Step Mechanism (Exam Favourite!):**\n     - Step 1: Protonation of alkene to form more stable **carbocation** (H⁺ + H₂O ⇌ H₃O⁺; CH₃–CH=CH₂ + H₃O⁺ → CH₃–CH⁺–CH₃ + H₂O).\n     - Step 2: Nucleophilic attack of H₂O on carbocation → protonated alcohol.\n     - Step 3: Deprotonation (loss of H⁺) → alcohol + H₃O⁺ regenerated.\n2. **Hydroboration–Oxidation (Anti-Markovnikov Addition):**\n   3CH₃–CH=CH₂ + ½(BH₃)₂ → (CH₃–CH₂–CH₂)₃B —(H₂O₂ / aq. NaOH)→ **3CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–OH (Propan-1-ol)** + B(OH)₃\n   - Result: –OH goes to the **less substituted (terminal)** carbon!\n\n### (b) By Reduction of Carbonyl Compounds & Carboxylic Acids\n- **Aldehyde → 1° alcohol:** R–CHO + H₂ (Pd/Ni) ya NaBH₄ ya LiAlH₄ → **R–CH₂OH**.\n- **Ketone → 2° alcohol:** R–CO–R' + NaBH₄ ya LiAlH₄ → **R–CH(OH)–R'**.\n- **Carboxylic acid / Ester → 1° alcohol:** Needs strong reducing agent **LiAlH₄** (NaBH₄ acids/esters ko reduce nahi karta!). Industrially, R–COOH → ester → catalytic hydrogenation (H₂ / catalyst) → R–CH₂OH (cheaper than LiAlH₄!).\n\n### (c) From Grignard Reagents (R–Mg–X + Carbonyl → Alcohol!) — SUPER IMPORTANT!\nStep 1: Nucleophilic addition in dry ether; Step 2: Acidic hydrolysis (H₂O / H⁺):\n1. **Methanal (HCHO) + R–Mg–X → 1° Alcohol** (R–CH₂OH)\n2. **Any other Aldehyde (R'–CHO) + R–Mg–X → 2° Alcohol** (R'–CH(OH)–R)\n3. **Any Ketone (R'–CO–R'') + R–Mg–X → 3° Alcohol** (R'R''C(OH)–R)"
