            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. Explain why ethanol (M = 46) has a much higher boiling point (351 K) than methoxymethane (M = 46, BP = 248 K).\n2. Why are lower alcohols soluble in water, while higher alcohols are insoluble?\n3. Arrange 1° (ethanol), 2° (propan-2-ol), and 3° (2-methylpropan-2-ol) alcohols in decreasing order of acidic strength. Give reason.\n4. Explain why phenol is more acidic than ethanol (give two reasons).\n5. Why does phenol react with aqueous NaOH, whereas ethanol does not?\n6. Explain why ortho-nitrophenol is more steam-volatile than para-nitrophenol.\n7. Arrange phenol, p-cresol, and p-nitrophenol in increasing order of pKₐ value! (Careful: higher pKₐ = weaker acid!)\n8. Why is p-nitrophenol slightly more acidic than o-nitrophenol?\n9. Why does simple phenol NOT give effervescence with NaHCO₃, whereas 2,4,6-trinitrophenol (picric acid) does?\n10. Arrange propan-1-ol, 2-methylpropan-2-ol, and butan-1-ol in increasing order of boiling point.\n\n---\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Ethanol molecules associate through strong **intermolecular hydrogen bonding** (between O–H groups), requiring much more heat energy to separate them. Methoxymethane has no O–H bond, so it cannot form intermolecular H-bonds.\n2. Lower alcohols form **hydrogen bonds** with water molecules. As the non-polar hydrophobic hydrocarbon chain grows longer, it disrupts water's H-bonding network and decreases solubility.\n3. Decreasing acidic strength: **Ethanol (1°) > Propan-2-ol (2°) > 2-Methylpropan-2-ol (3°)**. More electron-releasing (+I) alkyl groups increase electron density on oxygen, reducing O–H bond polarity and destabilising the alkoxide ion.\n4. (i) The **phenoxide ion (C₆H₅O⁻)** is strongly stabilised by **resonance** delocalisation of negative charge into the benzene ring (whereas ethoxide's charge is localised on oxygen). (ii) Phenol's **sp² carbon** is more electronegative than ethanol's sp³ carbon, increasing O–H polarity.\n5. Phenol (pKₐ ≈ 10) is a stronger acid than water (pKₐ ≈ 15.7), so NaOH readily deprotonates it to sodium phenoxide + H₂O. Ethanol (pKₐ ≈ 15.9) is weaker than water, so it does not form sodium ethoxide with aqueous NaOH.\n6. **o-Nitrophenol** has **intramolecular H-bonding** (weak intermolecular forces → low BP → steam volatile), whereas **p-nitrophenol** has **intermolecular H-bonding** (molecular association → high BP → less volatile).\n7. Acid strength order: p-nitrophenol > phenol > p-cresol. Since **smaller pKₐ = stronger acid**, increasing **pKₐ** order is:\n   **p-Nitrophenol < Phenol < p-Cresol**.\n   Jaanch: pKₐ values: p-nitrophenol (7.1) < phenol (10.0) < p-cresol (10.2) ✅\n8. In o-nitrophenol, **intramolecular hydrogen bonding** between the adjacent –OH and –NO₂ groups makes proton (H⁺) release slightly harder than in p-nitrophenol.\n9. Simple phenol (pKₐ 10) is weaker than carbonic acid H₂CO₃ (pKₐ 6.4), so it cannot displace CO₂ from NaHCO₃. In picric acid (pKₐ 0.71), three –NO₂ groups at o,o,p make it far stronger than H₂CO₃, so it briskly liberates CO₂ from NaHCO₃!\n10. Increasing BP: **2-Methylpropan-2-ol (356 K) < Propan-1-ol (370 K) < Butan-1-ol (391 K)** (branching lowers BP of tert-butanol even below straight-chain propan-1-ol!).\n\n---\n\nAb tum alcohols aur phenols ke boiling points, H-bonding aur acidity orders ke har conceptual trap ko aasani se paar kar sakte ho!\n\n**Agle topic mein:** Hum [Chemical Reactions of Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers](alcohols-phenols-and-ethers-part4.md) padhenge — jahan **Kolbe's reaction, Reimer–Tiemann reaction, Aspirin synthesis aur HI ether cleavage** aayenge!",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. Explain why ethanol (M = 46) has a much higher boiling point (351 K) than methoxymethane (M = 46, BP = 248 K).\n2. Why are lower alcohols soluble in water, while higher alcohols are insoluble?\n3. Arrange 1° (ethanol), 2° (propan-2-ol), and 3° (2-methylpropan-2-ol) alcohols in decreasing order of acidic strength. Give reason.\n4. Explain why phenol is more acidic than ethanol (give two reasons).\n5. Why does phenol react with aqueous NaOH, whereas ethanol does not?\n6. Explain why ortho-nitrophenol is more steam-volatile than para-nitrophenol.\n7. Arrange phenol, p-cresol, and p-nitrophenol in increasing order of pKₐ value! (Careful: higher pKₐ = weaker acid!)\n8. Why is p-nitrophenol slightly more acidic than o-nitrophenol?\n9. Why does simple phenol NOT give effervescence with NaHCO₃, whereas 2,4,6-trinitrophenol (picric acid) does?\n10. Arrange propan-1-ol, 2-methylpropan-2-ol, and butan-1-ol in increasing order of boiling point.\n\n---\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Ethanol molecules associate through strong **intermolecular hydrogen bonding** (between O–H groups), requiring much more heat energy to separate them. Methoxymethane has no O–H bond, so it cannot form intermolecular H-bonds.\n2. Lower alcohols form **hydrogen bonds** with water molecules. As the non-polar hydrophobic hydrocarbon chain grows longer, it disrupts water's H-bonding network and decreases solubility.\n3. Decreasing acidic strength: **Ethanol (1°) > Propan-2-ol (2°) > 2-Methylpropan-2-ol (3°)**. More electron-releasing (+I) alkyl groups increase electron density on oxygen, reducing O–H bond polarity and destabilising the alkoxide ion.\n4. (i) The **phenoxide ion (C₆H₅O⁻)** is strongly stabilised by **resonance** delocalisation of negative charge into the benzene ring (whereas ethoxide's charge is localised on oxygen). (ii) Phenol's **sp² carbon** is more electronegative than ethanol's sp³ carbon, increasing O–H polarity.\n5. Phenol (pKₐ ≈ 10) is a stronger acid than water (pKₐ ≈ 15.7), so NaOH readily deprotonates it to sodium phenoxide + H₂O. Ethanol (pKₐ ≈ 15.9) is weaker than water, so it does not form sodium ethoxide with aqueous NaOH.\n6. **o-Nitrophenol** has **intramolecular H-bonding** (weak intermolecular forces → low BP → steam volatile), whereas **p-nitrophenol** has **intermolecular H-bonding** (molecular association → high BP → less volatile).\n7. Acid strength order: p-nitrophenol > phenol > p-cresol. Since **smaller pKₐ = stronger acid**, increasing **pKₐ** order is:\n   **p-Nitrophenol < Phenol < p-Cresol**.\n   Jaanch: pKₐ values: p-nitrophenol (7.1) < phenol (10.0) < p-cresol (10.2) ✅\n8. In o-nitrophenol, **intramolecular hydrogen bonding** between the adjacent –OH and –NO₂ groups makes proton (H⁺) release slightly harder than in p-nitrophenol.\n9. Simple phenol (pKₐ 10) is weaker than carbonic acid H₂CO₃ (pKₐ 6.4), so it cannot displace CO₂ from NaHCO₃. In picric acid (pKₐ 0.71), three –NO₂ groups at o,o,p make it far stronger than H₂CO₃, so it briskly liberates CO₂ from NaHCO₃!\n10. Increasing BP: **2-Methylpropan-2-ol (356 K) < Propan-1-ol (370 K) < Butan-1-ol (391 K)** (branching lowers BP of tert-butanol even below straight-chain propan-1-ol!).\n\n---\n\nAb tum alcohols aur phenols ke boiling points, H-bonding aur acidity orders ke har conceptual trap ko aasani se paar kar sakte ho!\n\n**Agle topic mein:** Hum [Chemical Reactions of Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers](alcohols-phenols-and-ethers-part4.md) padhenge — jahan **Kolbe's reaction, Reimer–Tiemann reaction, Aspirin synthesis aur HI ether cleavage** aayenge!"
