          {
            "heading": "1. Chemical Reactions of Alcohols",
            "headingHinglish": "1. Chemical Reactions of Alcohols",
            "body": "### (a) Esterification & Acetylation (O–H Bond Cleavage)\n- R–OH + R'–COOH —(H⁺, reversible)→ R'–COOR + H₂O (conc. H₂SO₄ catalyst; water removed to push forward).\n- **Aspirin synthesis:** Salicylic acid + (CH₃CO)₂O —(H⁺)→ **Acetylsalicylic acid (Aspirin)** + CH₃COOH.\n\n### (b) Acid-Catalysed Dehydration to Alkenes (C–O Bond Cleavage)\n- **Ethanol:** CH₃CH₂OH —(conc. H₂SO₄, 443 K)→ CH₂=CH₂ + H₂O.\n- **2° Alcohol:** 85% H₃PO₄ at 440 K → alkene.\n- **3° Alcohol:** 20% H₃PO₄ at 358 K → alkene (easiest!).\n- **Ease of dehydration order:** **3° > 2° > 1°** (most stable carbocation forms fastest!).\n- **3-Step Mechanism (Ethanol → Ethene):**\n  1. Fast protonation: CH₃CH₂OH + H⁺ ⇌ CH₃CH₂–O⁺H₂\n  2. Slow C–O cleavage (RDS): CH₃CH₂–O⁺H₂ → CH₃CH₂⁺ + H₂O\n  3. Fast β-proton loss: CH₃CH₂⁺ → CH₂=CH₂ + H⁺\n\n### (c) Oxidation & Catalytic Dehydrogenation (Hot Cu at 573 K)\n| Alcohol Type | Mild Oxidiser (PCC / CrO₃) | Strong Oxidiser (KMnO₄ / K₂Cr₂O₇-H⁺) | Hot Cu at 573 K |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **1° (R–CH₂OH)** | **Aldehyde (R–CHO)** | **Carboxylic acid (R–COOH)** | **Aldehyde (R–CHO)** + H₂ |\n| **2° (R₂CH–OH)** | **Ketone (R₂C=O)** | **Ketone (R₂C=O)** (vigorous breaks C–C) | **Ketone (R₂C=O)** + H₂ |\n| **3° (R₃C–OH)** | No reaction | Vigorous → C–C cleavage (smaller acid/ketone) | **Alkene** (dehydration!) + H₂O |",
            "bodyHinglish": "### (a) Esterification & Acetylation (O–H Bond Cleavage)\n- R–OH + R'–COOH —(H⁺, reversible)→ R'–COOR + H₂O (conc. H₂SO₄ catalyst; water removed to push forward).\n- **Aspirin synthesis:** Salicylic acid + (CH₃CO)₂O —(H⁺)→ **Acetylsalicylic acid (Aspirin)** + CH₃COOH.\n\n### (b) Acid-Catalysed Dehydration to Alkenes (C–O Bond Cleavage)\n- **Ethanol:** CH₃CH₂OH —(conc. H₂SO₄, 443 K)→ CH₂=CH₂ + H₂O.\n- **2° Alcohol:** 85% H₃PO₄ at 440 K → alkene.\n- **3° Alcohol:** 20% H₃PO₄ at 358 K → alkene (easiest!).\n- **Ease of dehydration order:** **3° > 2° > 1°** (most stable carbocation forms fastest!).\n- **3-Step Mechanism (Ethanol → Ethene):**\n  1. Fast protonation: CH₃CH₂OH + H⁺ ⇌ CH₃CH₂–O⁺H₂\n  2. Slow C–O cleavage (RDS): CH₃CH₂–O⁺H₂ → CH₃CH₂⁺ + H₂O\n  3. Fast β-proton loss: CH₃CH₂⁺ → CH₂=CH₂ + H⁺\n\n### (c) Oxidation & Catalytic Dehydrogenation (Hot Cu at 573 K)\n| Alcohol Type | Mild Oxidiser (PCC / CrO₃) | Strong Oxidiser (KMnO₄ / K₂Cr₂O₇-H⁺) | Hot Cu at 573 K |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **1° (R–CH₂OH)** | **Aldehyde (R–CHO)** | **Carboxylic acid (R–COOH)** | **Aldehyde (R–CHO)** + H₂ |\n| **2° (R₂CH–OH)** | **Ketone (R₂C=O)** | **Ketone (R₂C=O)** (vigorous breaks C–C) | **Ketone (R₂C=O)** + H₂ |\n| **3° (R₃C–OH)** | No reaction | Vigorous → C–C cleavage (smaller acid/ketone) | **Alkene** (dehydration!) + H₂O |"
          },
          {
            "heading": "2. Chemical Reactions of Phenols",
            "headingHinglish": "2. Chemical Reactions of Phenols",
            "body": "–OH group benzene ring ko **strongly activate** karta hai (+R resonance effect) aur **ortho/para-directing** hota hai!\n\n### (a) Electrophilic Aromatic Substitution (EAS) — Solvent Matters!\n1. **Nitration:**\n   - **Dilute HNO₃ (298 K):** **o-Nitrophenol + p-Nitrophenol** (separated by steam distillation).\n   - **Conc. HNO₃:** **2,4,6-Trinitrophenol (Picric acid)** (poor yield due to oxidation; better made via phenol → phenol-2,4-disulphonic acid → conc. HNO₃!).\n2. **Bromination (SOLVENT EFFECT — SUPER IMPORTANT!):**\n   - **Br₂ in CS₂ or CHCl₃ (low-polarity solvent, 273 K):** Monobromination → **p-Bromophenol (major)** + o-bromophenol (no FeBr₃ catalyst needed!).\n   - **Br₂ in Water (polar solvent):** Phenol ionises to hyper-active phenoxide ion → **2,4,6-Tribromophenol (white precipitate!)**!\n\n### (b) Two Superstar Named Reactions of Phenol:\n1. **Kolbe's Reaction (Phenol → Salicylic Acid):**\n   C₆H₅–OH —(NaOH)→ C₆H₅–ONa —(1. CO₂, 2. H⁺)→ **2-Hydroxybenzoic acid (Salicylic acid)**\n2. **Reimer–Tiemann Reaction (Phenol → Salicylaldehyde):**\n   C₆H₅–OH + CHCl₃ + aq. NaOH → [substituted benzal chloride intermediate] —(NaOH, then H⁺)→ **2-Hydroxybenzaldehyde (Salicylaldehyde)**\n   *(Electrophile attacking the ring is **dichlorocarbene, :CCl₂**!)*\n\n### (c) Zinc Dust Distillation & Oxidation of Phenol:\n- **With Zinc dust (Δ):** **C₆H₅–OH + Zn —(Δ)→ C₆H₆ (Benzene) + ZnO** (removes –OH cleanly!).\n- **With Na₂Cr₂O₇ / H₂SO₄:** Phenol oxidises to **Benzoquinone (p-benzoquinone)**!",
            "bodyHinglish": "–OH group benzene ring ko **strongly activate** karta hai (+R resonance effect) aur **ortho/para-directing** hota hai!\n\n### (a) Electrophilic Aromatic Substitution (EAS) — Solvent Matters!\n1. **Nitration:**\n   - **Dilute HNO₃ (298 K):** **o-Nitrophenol + p-Nitrophenol** (separated by steam distillation).\n   - **Conc. HNO₃:** **2,4,6-Trinitrophenol (Picric acid)** (poor yield due to oxidation; better made via phenol → phenol-2,4-disulphonic acid → conc. HNO₃!).\n2. **Bromination (SOLVENT EFFECT — SUPER IMPORTANT!):**\n   - **Br₂ in CS₂ or CHCl₃ (low-polarity solvent, 273 K):** Monobromination → **p-Bromophenol (major)** + o-bromophenol (no FeBr₃ catalyst needed!).\n   - **Br₂ in Water (polar solvent):** Phenol ionises to hyper-active phenoxide ion → **2,4,6-Tribromophenol (white precipitate!)**!\n\n### (b) Two Superstar Named Reactions of Phenol:\n1. **Kolbe's Reaction (Phenol → Salicylic Acid):**\n   C₆H₅–OH —(NaOH)→ C₆H₅–ONa —(1. CO₂, 2. H⁺)→ **2-Hydroxybenzoic acid (Salicylic acid)**\n2. **Reimer–Tiemann Reaction (Phenol → Salicylaldehyde):**\n   C₆H₅–OH + CHCl₃ + aq. NaOH → [substituted benzal chloride intermediate] —(NaOH, then H⁺)→ **2-Hydroxybenzaldehyde (Salicylaldehyde)**\n   *(Electrophile attacking the ring is **dichlorocarbene, :CCl₂**!)*\n\n### (c) Zinc Dust Distillation & Oxidation of Phenol:\n- **With Zinc dust (Δ):** **C₆H₅–OH + Zn —(Δ)→ C₆H₆ (Benzene) + ZnO** (removes –OH cleanly!).\n- **With Na₂Cr₂O₇ / H₂SO₄:** Phenol oxidises to **Benzoquinone (p-benzoquinone)**!"
          },
          {
            "heading": "3. Chemical Reactions of Ethers (Cleavage by HX & EAS of Anisole)",
            "headingHinglish": "3. Chemical Reactions of Ethers (Cleavage by HX & EAS of Anisole)",
            "body": "### (a) Cleavage of C–O Bond by HI / HBr (Reactivity: HI > HBr > HCl)\n1. **Simple 1° / 2° Mixed Ethers (SN2 mechanism):**\n   **I⁻ attacks the SMALLER (less sterically hindered) alkyl group!**\n   - CH₃–O–CH₂CH₃ + HI (cold) → **CH₃–I** + **CH₃CH₂–OH**!\n2. **When ONE group is Tertiary (3°) or Benzylic (SN1 mechanism!):**\n   **3° carbocation forms first, so I⁻ goes to the TERTIARY group!**\n   - **(CH₃)₃C–O–CH₃ + HI → (CH₃)₃C–I + CH₃–OH**! *(Exam trap #1!)*\n3. **Alkyl Aryl Ethers (Anisole + HI):**\n   The Ar–O bond has partial double-bond character (resonance) and NEVER breaks!\n   - **C₆H₅–O–CH₃ + HI → C₆H₅–OH (Phenol) + CH₃–I** (ALWAYS Phenol + Alkyl iodide, never iodobenzene!).\n\n### (b) Electrophilic Substitution of Anisole (C₆H₅–OCH₃)\n–OCH₃ is **o,p-directing and activating** (para is always major!):\n- **Br₂ in ethanoic acid (no FeBr₃ needed!):** **p-Bromoanisole (90%, major)** + o-bromoanisole.\n- **Friedel–Crafts alkylation (CH₃Cl / anhyd. AlCl₃):** **4-Methoxytoluene (major)** + 2-methoxytoluene.\n- **Friedel–Crafts acylation (CH₃COCl / anhyd. AlCl₃):** **4-Methoxyacetophenone (major)**.\n- **Nitration (conc. HNO₃ + conc. H₂SO₄):** **4-Nitroanisole (major)** + 2-nitroanisole.",
            "bodyHinglish": "### (a) Cleavage of C–O Bond by HI / HBr (Reactivity: HI > HBr > HCl)\n1. **Simple 1° / 2° Mixed Ethers (SN2 mechanism):**\n   **I⁻ attacks the SMALLER (less sterically hindered) alkyl group!**\n   - CH₃–O–CH₂CH₃ + HI (cold) → **CH₃–I** + **CH₃CH₂–OH**!\n2. **When ONE group is Tertiary (3°) or Benzylic (SN1 mechanism!):**\n   **3° carbocation forms first, so I⁻ goes to the TERTIARY group!**\n   - **(CH₃)₃C–O–CH₃ + HI → (CH₃)₃C–I + CH₃–OH**! *(Exam trap #1!)*\n3. **Alkyl Aryl Ethers (Anisole + HI):**\n   The Ar–O bond has partial double-bond character (resonance) and NEVER breaks!\n   - **C₆H₅–O–CH₃ + HI → C₆H₅–OH (Phenol) + CH₃–I** (ALWAYS Phenol + Alkyl iodide, never iodobenzene!).\n\n### (b) Electrophilic Substitution of Anisole (C₆H₅–OCH₃)\n–OCH₃ is **o,p-directing and activating** (para is always major!):\n- **Br₂ in ethanoic acid (no FeBr₃ needed!):** **p-Bromoanisole (90%, major)** + o-bromoanisole.\n- **Friedel–Crafts alkylation (CH₃Cl / anhyd. AlCl₃):** **4-Methoxytoluene (major)** + 2-methoxytoluene.\n- **Friedel–Crafts acylation (CH₃COCl / anhyd. AlCl₃):** **4-Methoxyacetophenone (major)**.\n- **Nitration (conc. HNO₃ + conc. H₂SO₄):** **4-Nitroanisole (major)** + 2-nitroanisole."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Solved Examples",
            "headingHinglish": "Solved Examples",
            "body": "### Example 1: Predicting HI Cleavage Products of Three Ethers\nPredict the products of reacting 1 mole of cold HI with:\n(a) CH₃–CH₂–O–CH₃\n(b) (CH₃)₃C–O–CH₂–CH₃\n(c) C₆H₅–O–CH₂–CH₃\n\n**Solution:**\n(a) Both groups are 1° → **SN2**: I⁻ attacks smaller methyl group → **CH₃–I + CH₃CH₂–OH**.\n(b) One group is 3° → **SN1**: stable (CH₃)₃C⁺ forms first → **(CH₃)₃C–I + CH₃CH₂–OH**.\n(c) Aryl–O bond cannot break (partial double bond) → **C₆H₅–OH (phenol) + CH₃CH₂–I**.\n\nJaanch: SN2 (smaller → I), SN1 (3° → I), Aryl (Ar–OH always preserved!) ✅\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Phenol to Aspirin (2-Step Synthesis)\nWrite the two-step synthesis of **aspirin** starting from **phenol**.\n\n**Solution:**\n- **Step 1 (Kolbe's reaction):** C₆H₅–OH —(1. NaOH, 2. CO₂, 3. H⁺)→ **2-Hydroxybenzoic acid (Salicylic acid)**.\n- **Step 2 (Acetylation):** Salicylic acid + (CH₃CO)₂O —(H⁺)→ **2-Acetoxybenzoic acid (Aspirin)** + CH₃COOH.\n\nJaanch: Phenolic –OH gets acetylated to –OCOCH₃ while –COOH remains intact ✅\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: Vapour-Phase Action of Hot Copper (573 K)\nWhat happens when vapours of (a) propan-1-ol, (b) propan-2-ol, and (c) 2-methylpropan-2-ol are passed over heated copper at 573 K?\n\n**Solution:**\n- (a) 1° alcohol → **dehydrogenation** → **Propanal (CH₃CH₂CHO)** + H₂.\n- (b) 2° alcohol → **dehydrogenation** → **Propanone (CH₃COCH₃)** + H₂.\n- (c) 3° alcohol (no α-H!) → **dehydration** → **2-Methylpropene ((CH₃)₂C=CH₂)** + H₂O.\n\nJaanch: 1° → aldehyde, 2° → ketone, 3° → alkene ✅",
            "bodyHinglish": "### Example 1: Predicting HI Cleavage Products of Three Ethers\nPredict the products of reacting 1 mole of cold HI with:\n(a) CH₃–CH₂–O–CH₃\n(b) (CH₃)₃C–O–CH₂–CH₃\n(c) C₆H₅–O–CH₂–CH₃\n\n**Solution:**\n(a) Both groups are 1° → **SN2**: I⁻ attacks smaller methyl group → **CH₃–I + CH₃CH₂–OH**.\n(b) One group is 3° → **SN1**: stable (CH₃)₃C⁺ forms first → **(CH₃)₃C–I + CH₃CH₂–OH**.\n(c) Aryl–O bond cannot break (partial double bond) → **C₆H₅–OH (phenol) + CH₃CH₂–I**.\n\nJaanch: SN2 (smaller → I), SN1 (3° → I), Aryl (Ar–OH always preserved!) ✅\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Phenol to Aspirin (2-Step Synthesis)\nWrite the two-step synthesis of **aspirin** starting from **phenol**.\n\n**Solution:**\n- **Step 1 (Kolbe's reaction):** C₆H₅–OH —(1. NaOH, 2. CO₂, 3. H⁺)→ **2-Hydroxybenzoic acid (Salicylic acid)**.\n- **Step 2 (Acetylation):** Salicylic acid + (CH₃CO)₂O —(H⁺)→ **2-Acetoxybenzoic acid (Aspirin)** + CH₃COOH.\n\nJaanch: Phenolic –OH gets acetylated to –OCOCH₃ while –COOH remains intact ✅\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: Vapour-Phase Action of Hot Copper (573 K)\nWhat happens when vapours of (a) propan-1-ol, (b) propan-2-ol, and (c) 2-methylpropan-2-ol are passed over heated copper at 573 K?\n\n**Solution:**\n- (a) 1° alcohol → **dehydrogenation** → **Propanal (CH₃CH₂CHO)** + H₂.\n- (b) 2° alcohol → **dehydrogenation** → **Propanone (CH₃COCH₃)** + H₂.\n- (c) 3° alcohol (no α-H!) → **dehydration** → **2-Methylpropene ((CH₃)₂C=CH₂)** + H₂O.\n\nJaanch: 1° → aldehyde, 2° → ketone, 3° → alkene ✅"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Common Exam Mistakes (Watch Out!)",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam Galtiyan (Common Mistakes)",
            "body": "1. **(CH₃)₃C–O–CH₃ + HI mein SN2 lagakar CH₃I + (CH₃)₃COH bana dena:**\n   - *Galat:* Chhota group CH₃ hai toh CH₃I banega.\n   - *Sahi:* Jaise hi **3° ya benzylic group** dikhe, mechanism **SN1** mein switch ho jata hai → **(CH₃)₃C–I + CH₃OH** banta hai!\n\n2. **Phenol + Br₂/H₂O aur Phenol + Br₂/CS₂ ke products mix kar dena:**\n   - *Galat:* Dono mein p-bromophenol banta hai.\n   - *Sahi:* **Br₂ / H₂O (polar)** → **2,4,6-tribromophenol (white ppt)**; **Br₂ / CS₂ (non-polar)** → **p-bromophenol (major)**!\n\n3. **Kolbe aur Reimer–Tiemann ke products ulta kar dena:**\n   - *Galat:* Kolbe se salicylaldehyde banta hai.\n   - *Sahi:* **Kolbe (CO₂)** → **Salicylic acid (–COOH)**; **Reimer–Tiemann (CHCl₃/NaOH)** → **Salicylaldehyde (–CHO)**!",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. **(CH₃)₃C–O–CH₃ + HI mein SN2 lagakar CH₃I + (CH₃)₃COH bana dena:**\n   - *Galat:* Chhota group CH₃ hai toh CH₃I banega.\n   - *Sahi:* Jaise hi **3° ya benzylic group** dikhe, mechanism **SN1** mein switch ho jata hai → **(CH₃)₃C–I + CH₃OH** banta hai!\n\n2. **Phenol + Br₂/H₂O aur Phenol + Br₂/CS₂ ke products mix kar dena:**\n   - *Galat:* Dono mein p-bromophenol banta hai.\n   - *Sahi:* **Br₂ / H₂O (polar)** → **2,4,6-tribromophenol (white ppt)**; **Br₂ / CS₂ (non-polar)** → **p-bromophenol (major)**!\n\n3. **Kolbe aur Reimer–Tiemann ke products ulta kar dena:**\n   - *Galat:* Kolbe se salicylaldehyde banta hai.\n   - *Sahi:* **Kolbe (CO₂)** → **Salicylic acid (–COOH)**; **Reimer–Tiemann (CHCl₃/NaOH)** → **Salicylaldehyde (–CHO)**!"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- **Alcohols:** Dehydration ease **3° > 2° > 1°**; PCC stops 1° alcohol at **aldehyde**; hot Cu (573 K): 1° → aldehyde, 2° → ketone, 3° → alkene.\n- **Phenols:** Br₂/H₂O → 2,4,6-tribromophenol (white ppt); Br₂/CS₂ → p-bromophenol; **Kolbe** (NaOH/CO₂/H⁺) → salicylic acid; **Reimer–Tiemann** (CHCl₃/NaOH/H⁺) → salicylaldehyde; Zn dust → benzene.\n- **Ethers + HI:** 1°/2° → SN2 (I⁻ to smaller R); 3°/benzylic → SN1 (I⁻ to 3°/benzyl); Anisole → **Phenol + CH₃I**.",
            "bodyHinglish": "- **Alcohols:** Dehydration ease **3° > 2° > 1°**; PCC stops 1° alcohol at **aldehyde**; hot Cu (573 K): 1° → aldehyde, 2° → ketone, 3° → alkene.\n- **Phenols:** Br₂/H₂O → 2,4,6-tribromophenol (white ppt); Br₂/CS₂ → p-bromophenol; **Kolbe** (NaOH/CO₂/H⁺) → salicylic acid; **Reimer–Tiemann** (CHCl₃/NaOH/H⁺) → salicylaldehyde; Zn dust → benzene.\n- **Ethers + HI:** 1°/2° → SN2 (I⁻ to smaller R); 3°/benzylic → SN1 (I⁻ to 3°/benzyl); Anisole → **Phenol + CH₃I**."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. Write the 3-step mechanism for acid-catalysed dehydration of ethanol to ethene at 443 K.\n2. Why is PCC (pyridinium chlorochromate) preferred over KMnO₄ for converting propan-1-ol to propanal?\n3. Write the chemical equations for: (a) Kolbe's reaction, (b) Reimer–Tiemann reaction.\n4. Explain why bromination of phenol in water gives 2,4,6-tribromophenol, whereas in CS₂ at 273 K it gives mainly p-bromophenol.\n5. What happens when phenol is heated with: (a) zinc dust, (b) Na₂Cr₂O₇ + conc. H₂SO₄?\n6. Predict the products of reacting HI (1 mole) with: (a) C₆H₅–O–CH₃, (b) (CH₃)₃C–O–CH₃, (c) CH₃–O–CH₂CH₂CH₃.\n7. Why does anisole react with HI to give phenol and iodomethane rather than iodobenzene and methanol?\n8. Complete the reactions of anisole with: (a) Br₂ in ethanoic acid, (b) CH₃COCl / anhyd. AlCl₃.\n9. Name the electrophile generated in the Reimer–Tiemann reaction.\n10. Convert: (a) Phenol to salicylic acid, (b) Phenol to benzene, (c) Propan-2-ol to propanone.\n\n---\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Step 1: CH₃CH₂OH + H⁺ ⇌ CH₃CH₂–O⁺H₂ (protonation).\n   Step 2: CH₃CH₂–O⁺H₂ → CH₃CH₂⁺ + H₂O (slow, RDS: carbocation).\n   Step 3: CH₃CH₂⁺ → **CH₂=CH₂** + H⁺ (deprotonation).\n2. KMnO₄ is a strong oxidising agent that oxidises propan-1-ol all the way to propanoic acid. **PCC** is a mild, anhydrous oxidising agent that stops oxidation cleanly at **propanal**.\n3. (a) **Kolbe:** C₆H₅OH —(NaOH)→ C₆H₅ONa —(1. CO₂, 2. H⁺)→ **2-hydroxybenzoic acid (salicylic acid)**.\n   (b) **Reimer–Tiemann:** C₆H₅OH + CHCl₃ + aq. NaOH —(then H⁺)→ **2-hydroxybenzaldehyde (salicylaldehyde)**.\n4. In **polar water**, phenol ionises into the highly activating **phenoxide ion (C₆H₅O⁻)**, causing rapid trisubstitution to white **2,4,6-tribromophenol**. In **low-polarity CS₂**, ionisation is suppressed and Br₂ polarisation is weaker, giving clean monobromination (**p-bromophenol**).\n5. (a) **Benzene (C₆H₆)** + ZnO.\n   (b) **p-Benzoquinone** (conjugated diketone).\n6. (a) **C₆H₅OH + CH₃I**\n   (b) **(CH₃)₃C–I + CH₃OH** (SN1 via 3° carbocation!)\n   (c) **CH₃I + CH₃CH₂CH₂OH** (SN2 at smaller methyl!)\n7. Because the **C(aryl)–O bond** in anisole has **partial double-bond character** (resonance) and an sp² carbon, making it much stronger than the sp³ O–CH₃ bond; hence only O–CH₃ cleaves to give phenol + CH₃I.\n8. (a) **4-Bromoanisole (major)** + 2-bromoanisole.\n   (b) **4-Methoxyacetophenone (major)** + 2-methoxyacetophenone.\n9. **Dichlorocarbene (:CCl₂)** (neutral electron-deficient species formed from CHCl₃ + OH⁻).\n10. (a) Phenol —(1. NaOH, 2. CO₂, 3. H⁺ — Kolbe)→ **Salicylic acid**.\n    (b) Phenol + Zn dust —(Δ)→ **Benzene** + ZnO.\n    (c) Propan-2-ol —(CrO₃ or Cu / 573 K)→ **Propanone**.\n\n---\n\nAb tum alcohols, phenols aur ethers ke sabhi named reactions aur cleavage mechanisms master kar chuke ho!\n\n**Agle topic mein:** Hum [Commercially Important Alcohols, Uses and Identification Tests](alcohols-phenols-and-ethers-part5.md) padhenge — jahan **Lucas Test, Neutral FeCl₃ Test aur Ethanol Fermentation** seekhenge!",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. Write the 3-step mechanism for acid-catalysed dehydration of ethanol to ethene at 443 K.\n2. Why is PCC (pyridinium chlorochromate) preferred over KMnO₄ for converting propan-1-ol to propanal?\n3. Write the chemical equations for: (a) Kolbe's reaction, (b) Reimer–Tiemann reaction.\n4. Explain why bromination of phenol in water gives 2,4,6-tribromophenol, whereas in CS₂ at 273 K it gives mainly p-bromophenol.\n5. What happens when phenol is heated with: (a) zinc dust, (b) Na₂Cr₂O₇ + conc. H₂SO₄?\n6. Predict the products of reacting HI (1 mole) with: (a) C₆H₅–O–CH₃, (b) (CH₃)₃C–O–CH₃, (c) CH₃–O–CH₂CH₂CH₃.\n7. Why does anisole react with HI to give phenol and iodomethane rather than iodobenzene and methanol?\n8. Complete the reactions of anisole with: (a) Br₂ in ethanoic acid, (b) CH₃COCl / anhyd. AlCl₃.\n9. Name the electrophile generated in the Reimer–Tiemann reaction.\n10. Convert: (a) Phenol to salicylic acid, (b) Phenol to benzene, (c) Propan-2-ol to propanone.\n\n---\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Step 1: CH₃CH₂OH + H⁺ ⇌ CH₃CH₂–O⁺H₂ (protonation).\n   Step 2: CH₃CH₂–O⁺H₂ → CH₃CH₂⁺ + H₂O (slow, RDS: carbocation).\n   Step 3: CH₃CH₂⁺ → **CH₂=CH₂** + H⁺ (deprotonation).\n2. KMnO₄ is a strong oxidising agent that oxidises propan-1-ol all the way to propanoic acid. **PCC** is a mild, anhydrous oxidising agent that stops oxidation cleanly at **propanal**.\n3. (a) **Kolbe:** C₆H₅OH —(NaOH)→ C₆H₅ONa —(1. CO₂, 2. H⁺)→ **2-hydroxybenzoic acid (salicylic acid)**.\n   (b) **Reimer–Tiemann:** C₆H₅OH + CHCl₃ + aq. NaOH —(then H⁺)→ **2-hydroxybenzaldehyde (salicylaldehyde)**.\n4. In **polar water**, phenol ionises into the highly activating **phenoxide ion (C₆H₅O⁻)**, causing rapid trisubstitution to white **2,4,6-tribromophenol**. In **low-polarity CS₂**, ionisation is suppressed and Br₂ polarisation is weaker, giving clean monobromination (**p-bromophenol**).\n5. (a) **Benzene (C₆H₆)** + ZnO.\n   (b) **p-Benzoquinone** (conjugated diketone).\n6. (a) **C₆H₅OH + CH₃I**\n   (b) **(CH₃)₃C–I + CH₃OH** (SN1 via 3° carbocation!)\n   (c) **CH₃I + CH₃CH₂CH₂OH** (SN2 at smaller methyl!)\n7. Because the **C(aryl)–O bond** in anisole has **partial double-bond character** (resonance) and an sp² carbon, making it much stronger than the sp³ O–CH₃ bond; hence only O–CH₃ cleaves to give phenol + CH₃I.\n8. (a) **4-Bromoanisole (major)** + 2-bromoanisole.\n   (b) **4-Methoxyacetophenone (major)** + 2-methoxyacetophenone.\n9. **Dichlorocarbene (:CCl₂)** (neutral electron-deficient species formed from CHCl₃ + OH⁻).\n10. (a) Phenol —(1. NaOH, 2. CO₂, 3. H⁺ — Kolbe)→ **Salicylic acid**.\n    (b) Phenol + Zn dust —(Δ)→ **Benzene** + ZnO.\n    (c) Propan-2-ol —(CrO₃ or Cu / 573 K)→ **Propanone**.\n\n---\n\nAb tum alcohols, phenols aur ethers ke sabhi named reactions aur cleavage mechanisms master kar chuke ho!\n\n**Agle topic mein:** Hum [Commercially Important Alcohols, Uses and Identification Tests](alcohols-phenols-and-ethers-part5.md) padhenge — jahan **Lucas Test, Neutral FeCl₃ Test aur Ethanol Fermentation** seekhenge!"
          }
