            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. Write the balanced chemical equation and conditions for the industrial manufacture of methanol.\n2. Write the two enzymatic reactions involved in preparing ethanol by fermentation of sucrose (molasses).\n3. What is denatured alcohol? Name the substances commonly added to denature ethanol.\n4. What is Lucas reagent? Explain how it distinguishes between 1°, 2°, and 3° alcohols.\n5. Why does 2-methylpropan-2-ol give immediate turbidity with Lucas reagent, whereas butan-1-ol does not?\n6. Give a simple chemical test to distinguish between:\n   (a) Phenol and Benzyl alcohol\n   (b) Pentan-2-ol and Pentan-3-ol\n   (c) Butan-1-ol and 2-Methylpropan-2-ol\n7. Name the ONLY primary alcohol that gives a positive iodoform test. Write its structure.\n8. Why does benzyl alcohol (a 1° alcohol) give immediate turbidity in the Lucas test?\n9. State two major industrial uses each of methanol and ethanol.\n10. An alcohol C₃H₈O gives no turbidity with Lucas reagent at room temperature and fails the iodoform test. Identify the alcohol.\n\n---\n\n### Answers\n\n1. **CO + 2H₂ —(ZnO–Cr₂O₃ catalyst, 200–300 atm, 573–673 K)→ CH₃OH**.\n2. Step 1: C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁ + H₂O —(invertase)→ C₆H₁₂O₆ (glucose) + C₆H₁₂O₆ (fructose).\n   Step 2: C₆H₁₂O₆ —(zymase)→ 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂↑.\n3. **Denatured alcohol** is commercial ethanol rendered unfit for human consumption by adding poisonous/foul-smelling substances — typically **copper sulphate (CuSO₄)** (for blue colour) and **pyridine** (for foul smell).\n4. **Lucas reagent** = conc. HCl + anhydrous ZnCl₂. It converts alcohols to insoluble R–Cl (turbidity): **3°** gives immediate turbidity, **2°** gives turbidity in ~5 minutes, and **1°** gives no turbidity at room temperature.\n5. Because 2-methylpropan-2-ol (3°) forms a very stable **3° carbocation** instantly via SN1, whereas butan-1-ol (1°) cannot easily form an unstable 1° carbocation at room temperature.\n6. (a) **Neutral FeCl₃ test:** **Phenol** gives violet colour; **benzyl alcohol** gives no colour.\n   (b) **Iodoform test (I₂ + NaOH):** **Pentan-2-ol** (has CH₃CH(OH)–) gives yellow CHI₃ ppt; **pentan-3-ol** gives no ppt.\n   (c) **Lucas test:** **2-Methylpropan-2-ol (3°)** gives immediate turbidity; **butan-1-ol (1°)** gives no turbidity at room temp.\n7. **Ethanol (CH₃–CH₂–OH)** — it is the only 1° alcohol containing the CH₃–CH(OH)– structural unit!\n8. Because benzyl alcohol forms a **resonance-stabilised benzyl carbocation (C₆H₅–CH₂⁺)**, which is as stable as a 3° carbocation, causing rapid SN1 reaction with Lucas reagent.\n9. **Methanol:** Solvent for paints/varnishes, manufacture of formaldehyde (HCHO). **Ethanol:** Solvent in paint/pharma industries, fuel additive / hand sanitiser.\n10. Isomers of C₃H₈O alcohols are propan-1-ol (1°) and propan-2-ol (2°). No Lucas turbidity + negative iodoform → **Propan-1-ol (CH₃CH₂CH₂OH)**.\n    Jaanch: Propan-2-ol would be positive in both Lucas (~5 min) and iodoform ✅\n\n---\n\nAb tum commercial alcohols aur sabhi distinction tests (Lucas, FeCl₃, Iodoform) bilkul pakke kar chuke ho!\n\n**Agle topic mein:** [Sab Kuch Mila Kar: Mixed Conversions & Chapter Wrap-up](alcohols-phenols-and-ethers-part6.md) mein hum poore chapter ke multi-step organic conversions aur wrap-up table complete karenge!",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. Write the balanced chemical equation and conditions for the industrial manufacture of methanol.\n2. Write the two enzymatic reactions involved in preparing ethanol by fermentation of sucrose (molasses).\n3. What is denatured alcohol? Name the substances commonly added to denature ethanol.\n4. What is Lucas reagent? Explain how it distinguishes between 1°, 2°, and 3° alcohols.\n5. Why does 2-methylpropan-2-ol give immediate turbidity with Lucas reagent, whereas butan-1-ol does not?\n6. Give a simple chemical test to distinguish between:\n   (a) Phenol and Benzyl alcohol\n   (b) Pentan-2-ol and Pentan-3-ol\n   (c) Butan-1-ol and 2-Methylpropan-2-ol\n7. Name the ONLY primary alcohol that gives a positive iodoform test. Write its structure.\n8. Why does benzyl alcohol (a 1° alcohol) give immediate turbidity in the Lucas test?\n9. State two major industrial uses each of methanol and ethanol.\n10. An alcohol C₃H₈O gives no turbidity with Lucas reagent at room temperature and fails the iodoform test. Identify the alcohol.\n\n---\n\n### Answers\n\n1. **CO + 2H₂ —(ZnO–Cr₂O₃ catalyst, 200–300 atm, 573–673 K)→ CH₃OH**.\n2. Step 1: C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁ + H₂O —(invertase)→ C₆H₁₂O₆ (glucose) + C₆H₁₂O₆ (fructose).\n   Step 2: C₆H₁₂O₆ —(zymase)→ 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂↑.\n3. **Denatured alcohol** is commercial ethanol rendered unfit for human consumption by adding poisonous/foul-smelling substances — typically **copper sulphate (CuSO₄)** (for blue colour) and **pyridine** (for foul smell).\n4. **Lucas reagent** = conc. HCl + anhydrous ZnCl₂. It converts alcohols to insoluble R–Cl (turbidity): **3°** gives immediate turbidity, **2°** gives turbidity in ~5 minutes, and **1°** gives no turbidity at room temperature.\n5. Because 2-methylpropan-2-ol (3°) forms a very stable **3° carbocation** instantly via SN1, whereas butan-1-ol (1°) cannot easily form an unstable 1° carbocation at room temperature.\n6. (a) **Neutral FeCl₃ test:** **Phenol** gives violet colour; **benzyl alcohol** gives no colour.\n   (b) **Iodoform test (I₂ + NaOH):** **Pentan-2-ol** (has CH₃CH(OH)–) gives yellow CHI₃ ppt; **pentan-3-ol** gives no ppt.\n   (c) **Lucas test:** **2-Methylpropan-2-ol (3°)** gives immediate turbidity; **butan-1-ol (1°)** gives no turbidity at room temp.\n7. **Ethanol (CH₃–CH₂–OH)** — it is the only 1° alcohol containing the CH₃–CH(OH)– structural unit!\n8. Because benzyl alcohol forms a **resonance-stabilised benzyl carbocation (C₆H₅–CH₂⁺)**, which is as stable as a 3° carbocation, causing rapid SN1 reaction with Lucas reagent.\n9. **Methanol:** Solvent for paints/varnishes, manufacture of formaldehyde (HCHO). **Ethanol:** Solvent in paint/pharma industries, fuel additive / hand sanitiser.\n10. Isomers of C₃H₈O alcohols are propan-1-ol (1°) and propan-2-ol (2°). No Lucas turbidity + negative iodoform → **Propan-1-ol (CH₃CH₂CH₂OH)**.\n    Jaanch: Propan-2-ol would be positive in both Lucas (~5 min) and iodoform ✅\n\n---\n\nAb tum commercial alcohols aur sabhi distinction tests (Lucas, FeCl₃, Iodoform) bilkul pakke kar chuke ho!\n\n**Agle topic mein:** [Sab Kuch Mila Kar: Mixed Conversions & Chapter Wrap-up](alcohols-phenols-and-ethers-part6.md) mein hum poore chapter ke multi-step organic conversions aur wrap-up table complete karenge!"
