          {
            "heading": "1. Physical Properties & Reactivity in Nucleophilic Addition",
            "headingHinglish": "1. Physical Properties & Reactivity in Nucleophilic Addition",
            "body": "- **Boiling Point:** Aldehydes & Ketones > Alkanes & Ethers (strong C=O dipole–dipole), par **< Alcohols** (no self H-bonding!).\n- **Why are Aldehydes MORE reactive than Ketones towards Nucleophilic Addition? (2 Reasons!)**\n  1. **Steric Reason:** Ketone mein carbonyl carbon par **2 bulky alkyl groups** hote hain (zyada steric hindrance), jabki aldehyde mein sirf 1 alkyl aur 1 chhota H hota hai.\n  2. **Electronic Reason:** Ketone mein **2 electron-releasing (+I) alkyl groups** carbonyl carbon ka δ⁺ charge kam kar dete hain (kam electrophilic!).\n  - Reactivity order: **HCHO > CH₃CHO > CH₃CH₂CHO > CH₃COCH₃ > C₆H₅CHO > C₆H₅COCH₃** (benzaldehyde resonance ki wajah se aliphatic aldehydes se kam reactive hai!).",
            "bodyHinglish": "- **Boiling Point:** Aldehydes & Ketones > Alkanes & Ethers (strong C=O dipole–dipole), par **< Alcohols** (no self H-bonding!).\n- **Why are Aldehydes MORE reactive than Ketones towards Nucleophilic Addition? (2 Reasons!)**\n  1. **Steric Reason:** Ketone mein carbonyl carbon par **2 bulky alkyl groups** hote hain (zyada steric hindrance), jabki aldehyde mein sirf 1 alkyl aur 1 chhota H hota hai.\n  2. **Electronic Reason:** Ketone mein **2 electron-releasing (+I) alkyl groups** carbonyl carbon ka δ⁺ charge kam kar dete hain (kam electrophilic!).\n  - Reactivity order: **HCHO > CH₃CHO > CH₃CH₂CHO > CH₃COCH₃ > C₆H₅CHO > C₆H₅COCH₃** (benzaldehyde resonance ki wajah se aliphatic aldehydes se kam reactive hai!)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "2. Important Nucleophilic Addition Reactions",
            "headingHinglish": "2. Important Nucleophilic Addition Reactions",
            "body": "1. **Addition of HCN (base catalyst OH⁻):** Forms **Cyanohydrin** (R–CH(OH)–CN).\n2. **Addition of NaHSO₃:** Forms crystalline **Bisulphite adduct** (water-soluble; used to purify/separate aldehydes & methyl ketones! Bulky ketones don't react).\n3. **Addition of Alcohols (dry HCl gas):**\n   - Aldehyde + 1 eq. ROH → **Hemiacetal**; + 2nd eq. ROH → **Acetal** (gem-dialkoxy).\n   - Ketone + ethylene glycol (dry HCl) → **Cyclic Ketal** (used to protect ketones!).\n4. **Addition of Ammonia Derivatives (H₂N–Z, weakly acidic pH ~3.5) → C=N–Z + H₂O:**\n\n| Reagent (H₂N–Z) | Product (>C=N–Z) | Special Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| Hydroxylamine (NH₂–OH) | **Oxime** (>C=N–OH) | Derivative |\n| Hydrazine (NH₂–NH₂) | **Hydrazone** (>C=N–NH₂) | Wolff–Kishner step 1 |\n| Phenylhydrazine (C₆H₅NHNH₂) | **Phenylhydrazone** | Derivative |\n| **2,4-Dinitrophenylhydrazine (2,4-DNP)** | **2,4-DNP derivative** | **Yellow/orange/red ppt — confirmatory test for ALL aldehydes & ketones!** |\n| Semicarbazide (NH₂–NH–CO–NH₂) | **Semicarbazone** | Only –NH₂ NOT attached to C=O attacks (other is resonance-locked!) |\n| 1° Amine (R–NH₂) | **Imine (Schiff's base)** (>C=N–R) | Synthetic intermediate |",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. **Addition of HCN (base catalyst OH⁻):** Forms **Cyanohydrin** (R–CH(OH)–CN).\n2. **Addition of NaHSO₃:** Forms crystalline **Bisulphite adduct** (water-soluble; used to purify/separate aldehydes & methyl ketones! Bulky ketones don't react).\n3. **Addition of Alcohols (dry HCl gas):**\n   - Aldehyde + 1 eq. ROH → **Hemiacetal**; + 2nd eq. ROH → **Acetal** (gem-dialkoxy).\n   - Ketone + ethylene glycol (dry HCl) → **Cyclic Ketal** (used to protect ketones!).\n4. **Addition of Ammonia Derivatives (H₂N–Z, weakly acidic pH ~3.5) → C=N–Z + H₂O:**\n\n| Reagent (H₂N–Z) | Product (>C=N–Z) | Special Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| Hydroxylamine (NH₂–OH) | **Oxime** (>C=N–OH) | Derivative |\n| Hydrazine (NH₂–NH₂) | **Hydrazone** (>C=N–NH₂) | Wolff–Kishner step 1 |\n| Phenylhydrazine (C₆H₅NHNH₂) | **Phenylhydrazone** | Derivative |\n| **2,4-Dinitrophenylhydrazine (2,4-DNP)** | **2,4-DNP derivative** | **Yellow/orange/red ppt — confirmatory test for ALL aldehydes & ketones!** |\n| Semicarbazide (NH₂–NH–CO–NH₂) | **Semicarbazone** | Only –NH₂ NOT attached to C=O attacks (other is resonance-locked!) |\n| 1° Amine (R–NH₂) | **Imine (Schiff's base)** (>C=N–R) | Synthetic intermediate |"
          },
          {
            "heading": "3. Reduction & Oxidation (Distinction Tests!)",
            "headingHinglish": "3. Reduction & Oxidation (Distinction Tests!)",
            "body": "### (a) Reduction to Hydrocarbons (>C=O → >CH₂):\n- **Clemmensen:** >C=O —(Zn–Hg / conc. HCl)→ **>CH₂** + H₂O.\n- **Wolff–Kishner:** >C=O —(1. NH₂NH₂, 2. KOH / ethylene glycol, Δ)→ **>CH₂** + N₂↑.\n\n### (b) Oxidation & Golden Distinction Tests (100% Exam Questions!):\n- **Tollens' Test ([Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺ / OH⁻, warm):**\n  R–CHO + 2[Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺ + 3OH⁻ → R–COO⁻ + **2Ag↓ (Silver Mirror!)** + 2H₂O + 4NH₃.\n  - Positive: **ALL aldehydes** (aliphatic + benzaldehyde!). Negative: Ketones!\n- **Fehling's Test (Fehling A = aq. CuSO₄ + Fehling B = alk. Rochelle salt):**\n  R–CHO + 2Cu²⁺ + 5OH⁻ → R–COO⁻ + **Cu₂O↓ (Reddish-Brown ppt!)** + 3H₂O.\n  - Positive: **Aliphatic aldehydes ONLY!** Negative: **Aromatic aldehydes (benzaldehyde)** and Ketones!\n- **Iodoform (Haloform) Test (I₂ + NaOH, warm):**\n  For **Methyl Ketones (CH₃–CO–R)** AND **Ethanal (CH₃–CHO)** (only aldehyde!):\n  R–CO–CH₃ + 3I₂ + 4NaOH → **CHI₃↓ (Yellow ppt)** + R–COONa + 3NaI + 3H₂O!\n  - *(Bonus: Does NOT touch C=C double bonds!)*",
            "bodyHinglish": "### (a) Reduction to Hydrocarbons (>C=O → >CH₂):\n- **Clemmensen:** >C=O —(Zn–Hg / conc. HCl)→ **>CH₂** + H₂O.\n- **Wolff–Kishner:** >C=O —(1. NH₂NH₂, 2. KOH / ethylene glycol, Δ)→ **>CH₂** + N₂↑.\n\n### (b) Oxidation & Golden Distinction Tests (100% Exam Questions!):\n- **Tollens' Test ([Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺ / OH⁻, warm):**\n  R–CHO + 2[Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺ + 3OH⁻ → R–COO⁻ + **2Ag↓ (Silver Mirror!)** + 2H₂O + 4NH₃.\n  - Positive: **ALL aldehydes** (aliphatic + benzaldehyde!). Negative: Ketones!\n- **Fehling's Test (Fehling A = aq. CuSO₄ + Fehling B = alk. Rochelle salt):**\n  R–CHO + 2Cu²⁺ + 5OH⁻ → R–COO⁻ + **Cu₂O↓ (Reddish-Brown ppt!)** + 3H₂O.\n  - Positive: **Aliphatic aldehydes ONLY!** Negative: **Aromatic aldehydes (benzaldehyde)** and Ketones!\n- **Iodoform (Haloform) Test (I₂ + NaOH, warm):**\n  For **Methyl Ketones (CH₃–CO–R)** AND **Ethanal (CH₃–CHO)** (only aldehyde!):\n  R–CO–CH₃ + 3I₂ + 4NaOH → **CHI₃↓ (Yellow ppt)** + R–COONa + 3NaI + 3H₂O!\n  - *(Bonus: Does NOT touch C=C double bonds!)*"
          },
          {
            "heading": "4. Reactions Due to α-Hydrogen: Aldol vs Cannizzaro (SUPER IMPORTANT!)",
            "headingHinglish": "4. Reactions Due to α-Hydrogen: Aldol vs Cannizzaro (SUPER IMPORTANT!)",
            "body": "α-Hydrogen of aldehydes/ketones is **acidic** because the resulting **enolate ion** is resonance-stabilised (**⁻CH₂–CH=O ↔ CH₂=CH–O⁻**)!\n\n### (a) Aldol Condensation (REQUIRES at least 1 α-Hydrogen + **Dilute NaOH**!)\n- **Self-Aldol of Ethanal (2 moles):**\n  2CH₃–CHO —(dil. NaOH)→ **CH₃–CH(OH)–CH₂–CHO** (3-Hydroxybutanal — Aldol) —(Δ, −H₂O)→ **CH₃–CH=CH–CHO (But-2-enal)**!\n- **Self-Aldol of Propanone:**\n  2CH₃–CO–CH₃ —(Ba(OH)₂)→ (CH₃)₂C(OH)–CH₂–CO–CH₃ —(Δ, −H₂O)→ **(CH₃)₂C=CH–CO–CH₃ (4-Methylpent-3-en-2-one)**.\n- **Cross-Aldol:** Two different aldehydes/ketones with α-H give a mixture of **4 products** (2 self + 2 cross). If one has **NO α-H** (e.g., benzaldehyde + acetophenone), a single clean cross-aldol product forms!\n  C₆H₅–CHO + CH₃–CO–C₆H₅ —(dil. NaOH, 293 K)→ **C₆H₅–CH=CH–CO–C₆H₅ (1,3-Diphenylprop-2-en-1-one / Benzalacetophenone)**!\n\n### (b) Cannizzaro Reaction (ONLY Aldehydes with **NO α-Hydrogen** + **Conc. NaOH (50%)**!)\nSelf oxidation–reduction (**disproportionation**): 1 molecule oxidises to carboxylic acid salt, 1 molecule reduces to alcohol!\n- **2HCHO + conc. KOH → CH₃OH (Methanol) + HCOOK (Potassium formate)**\n- **2C₆H₅–CHO + conc. NaOH → C₆H₅–CH₂OH (Benzyl alcohol) + C₆H₅–COONa (Sodium benzoate)**\n\n### (c) Electrophilic Substitution of Benzaldehyde:\n–CHO is **meta-directing and deactivating** (−R, −I):\nC₆H₅–CHO + conc. HNO₃ / H₂SO₄ (273–283 K) → **m-Nitrobenzaldehyde (3-nitrobenzaldehyde)**.",
            "bodyHinglish": "α-Hydrogen of aldehydes/ketones is **acidic** because the resulting **enolate ion** is resonance-stabilised (**⁻CH₂–CH=O ↔ CH₂=CH–O⁻**)!\n\n### (a) Aldol Condensation (REQUIRES at least 1 α-Hydrogen + **Dilute NaOH**!)\n- **Self-Aldol of Ethanal (2 moles):**\n  2CH₃–CHO —(dil. NaOH)→ **CH₃–CH(OH)–CH₂–CHO** (3-Hydroxybutanal — Aldol) —(Δ, −H₂O)→ **CH₃–CH=CH–CHO (But-2-enal)**!\n- **Self-Aldol of Propanone:**\n  2CH₃–CO–CH₃ —(Ba(OH)₂)→ (CH₃)₂C(OH)–CH₂–CO–CH₃ —(Δ, −H₂O)→ **(CH₃)₂C=CH–CO–CH₃ (4-Methylpent-3-en-2-one)**.\n- **Cross-Aldol:** Two different aldehydes/ketones with α-H give a mixture of **4 products** (2 self + 2 cross). If one has **NO α-H** (e.g., benzaldehyde + acetophenone), a single clean cross-aldol product forms!\n  C₆H₅–CHO + CH₃–CO–C₆H₅ —(dil. NaOH, 293 K)→ **C₆H₅–CH=CH–CO–C₆H₅ (1,3-Diphenylprop-2-en-1-one / Benzalacetophenone)**!\n\n### (b) Cannizzaro Reaction (ONLY Aldehydes with **NO α-Hydrogen** + **Conc. NaOH (50%)**!)\nSelf oxidation–reduction (**disproportionation**): 1 molecule oxidises to carboxylic acid salt, 1 molecule reduces to alcohol!\n- **2HCHO + conc. KOH → CH₃OH (Methanol) + HCOOK (Potassium formate)**\n- **2C₆H₅–CHO + conc. NaOH → C₆H₅–CH₂OH (Benzyl alcohol) + C₆H₅–COONa (Sodium benzoate)**\n\n### (c) Electrophilic Substitution of Benzaldehyde:\n–CHO is **meta-directing and deactivating** (−R, −I):\nC₆H₅–CHO + conc. HNO₃ / H₂SO₄ (273–283 K) → **m-Nitrobenzaldehyde (3-nitrobenzaldehyde)**."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Solved Examples",
            "headingHinglish": "Solved Examples",
            "body": "### Example 1: Distinguishing Three Carbonyl Pairs Chemically\nGive ONE chemical test to distinguish between:\n(a) Propanal and Propanone\n(b) Ethanal and Propanal\n(c) Benzaldehyde and Propanal\n\n**Solution:**\n(a) **Tollens' test (or Fehling's test):** **Propanal** gives a silver mirror (or red-brown Cu₂O ppt); **Propanone** gives NO reaction. (Or Iodoform test: propanone gives yellow CHI₃ ppt; propanal does not!)\n(b) **Iodoform test (I₂ + NaOH):** **Ethanal (CH₃CHO)** gives a **yellow precipitate of CHI₃**; **Propanal (CH₃CH₂CHO)** does NOT.\n(c) **Fehling's test:** **Propanal** (aliphatic aldehyde) gives a **reddish-brown precipitate of Cu₂O**; **Benzaldehyde** (aromatic aldehyde) gives NO reaction!\n\nJaanch: Every test gives an unambiguous positive vs negative visual result ✅\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Classifying Compounds into Aldol vs Cannizzaro\nWhich of the following will undergo (i) Aldol condensation, (ii) Cannizzaro reaction, (iii) Neither?\nMethanal, 2-Methylpentanal, Benzaldehyde, Benzophenone, 2,2-Dimethylbutanal, Cyclohexanone.\n\n**Solution:**\n- **Aldol condensation (needs α-H):** **2-Methylpentanal** (1 α-H), **Cyclohexanone** (4 α-H).\n- **Cannizzaro reaction (aldehyde with NO α-H):** **Methanal (HCHO)**, **Benzaldehyde (C₆H₅CHO)**, **2,2-Dimethylbutanal ((CH₃)₂C(C₂H₅)–CHO)**.\n- **Neither:** **Benzophenone (C₆H₅–CO–C₆H₅)** (ketone with no α-H!).\n\nJaanch: α-H count checked for all 6 compounds ✅\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: Choosing Between Clemmensen and Wolff–Kishner Reduction\nYou want to reduce **HO–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃** to **HO–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃** without destroying the –OH group. Should you use Clemmensen or Wolff–Kishner reduction?\n\n**Solution:**\nUse **Wolff–Kishner reduction (NH₂NH₂ + KOH / ethylene glycol, Δ)**! Clemmensen reduction uses concentrated HCl, which would protonate the acid-sensitive –OH group and convert it into –Cl or cause dehydration to an alkene. Basic Wolff–Kishner leaves –OH untouched!\n\nJaanch: Acid-sensitive group (–OH, acetal) → Wolff–Kishner; Base-sensitive group (–Br, ester) → Clemmensen ✅",
            "bodyHinglish": "### Example 1: Distinguishing Three Carbonyl Pairs Chemically\nGive ONE chemical test to distinguish between:\n(a) Propanal and Propanone\n(b) Ethanal and Propanal\n(c) Benzaldehyde and Propanal\n\n**Solution:**\n(a) **Tollens' test (or Fehling's test):** **Propanal** gives a silver mirror (or red-brown Cu₂O ppt); **Propanone** gives NO reaction. (Or Iodoform test: propanone gives yellow CHI₃ ppt; propanal does not!)\n(b) **Iodoform test (I₂ + NaOH):** **Ethanal (CH₃CHO)** gives a **yellow precipitate of CHI₃**; **Propanal (CH₃CH₂CHO)** does NOT.\n(c) **Fehling's test:** **Propanal** (aliphatic aldehyde) gives a **reddish-brown precipitate of Cu₂O**; **Benzaldehyde** (aromatic aldehyde) gives NO reaction!\n\nJaanch: Every test gives an unambiguous positive vs negative visual result ✅\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Classifying Compounds into Aldol vs Cannizzaro\nWhich of the following will undergo (i) Aldol condensation, (ii) Cannizzaro reaction, (iii) Neither?\nMethanal, 2-Methylpentanal, Benzaldehyde, Benzophenone, 2,2-Dimethylbutanal, Cyclohexanone.\n\n**Solution:**\n- **Aldol condensation (needs α-H):** **2-Methylpentanal** (1 α-H), **Cyclohexanone** (4 α-H).\n- **Cannizzaro reaction (aldehyde with NO α-H):** **Methanal (HCHO)**, **Benzaldehyde (C₆H₅CHO)**, **2,2-Dimethylbutanal ((CH₃)₂C(C₂H₅)–CHO)**.\n- **Neither:** **Benzophenone (C₆H₅–CO–C₆H₅)** (ketone with no α-H!).\n\nJaanch: α-H count checked for all 6 compounds ✅\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: Choosing Between Clemmensen and Wolff–Kishner Reduction\nYou want to reduce **HO–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃** to **HO–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃** without destroying the –OH group. Should you use Clemmensen or Wolff–Kishner reduction?\n\n**Solution:**\nUse **Wolff–Kishner reduction (NH₂NH₂ + KOH / ethylene glycol, Δ)**! Clemmensen reduction uses concentrated HCl, which would protonate the acid-sensitive –OH group and convert it into –Cl or cause dehydration to an alkene. Basic Wolff–Kishner leaves –OH untouched!\n\nJaanch: Acid-sensitive group (–OH, acetal) → Wolff–Kishner; Base-sensitive group (–Br, ester) → Clemmensen ✅"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Common Exam Mistakes (Watch Out!)",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam Galtiyan (Common Mistakes)",
            "body": "1. **Benzaldehyde ko Fehling's test positive bol dena:**\n   - *Galat:* Benzaldehyde ek aldehyde hai toh Fehling's test dega.\n   - *Sahi:* Benzaldehyde **Tollens' test deta hai**, par **Fehling's test NAHI deta**! (Aromatic aldehydes ko aliphatic aldehydes se alag karne ka yahi best test hai!)\n\n2. **Aldol aur Cannizzaro mein NaOH ki concentration aur α-H condition ulta kar dena:**\n   - *Galat:* HCHO dil. NaOH se aldol deta hai.\n   - *Sahi:* **Aldol** = **α-H present** + **dilute NaOH**; **Cannizzaro** = **NO α-H** + **concentrated NaOH (50%)**!\n\n3. **Semicarbazide (H₂N–NH–CO–NH₂) ke dono –NH₂ ko barabar reactive maan lena:**\n   - *Galat:* C=O ke bagal wala –NH₂ carbonyl par attack karta hai.\n   - *Sahi:* C=O ke bagal wale –NH₂ ka lone pair amide resonance mein busy rehta hai → sirf **door wala (hydrazine-end) –NH₂** attack karta hai!",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. **Benzaldehyde ko Fehling's test positive bol dena:**\n   - *Galat:* Benzaldehyde ek aldehyde hai toh Fehling's test dega.\n   - *Sahi:* Benzaldehyde **Tollens' test deta hai**, par **Fehling's test NAHI deta**! (Aromatic aldehydes ko aliphatic aldehydes se alag karne ka yahi best test hai!)\n\n2. **Aldol aur Cannizzaro mein NaOH ki concentration aur α-H condition ulta kar dena:**\n   - *Galat:* HCHO dil. NaOH se aldol deta hai.\n   - *Sahi:* **Aldol** = **α-H present** + **dilute NaOH**; **Cannizzaro** = **NO α-H** + **concentrated NaOH (50%)**!\n\n3. **Semicarbazide (H₂N–NH–CO–NH₂) ke dono –NH₂ ko barabar reactive maan lena:**\n   - *Galat:* C=O ke bagal wala –NH₂ carbonyl par attack karta hai.\n   - *Sahi:* C=O ke bagal wale –NH₂ ka lone pair amide resonance mein busy rehta hai → sirf **door wala (hydrazine-end) –NH₂** attack karta hai!"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- **Nucleophilic Addition Reactivity:** HCHO > RCHO > RCOR' (less steric hindrance + less +I effect).\n- **2,4-DNP test:** Positive for ALL aldehydes & ketones (yellow/orange/red ppt).\n- **Tollens':** ALL aldehydes (silver mirror). **Fehling's:** Aliphatic aldehydes ONLY (red-brown Cu₂O). **Iodoform:** CH₃CO– & CH₃CHO (yellow CHI₃).\n- **>C=O → >CH₂:** Clemmensen (Zn–Hg/HCl) or Wolff–Kishner (NH₂NH₂/KOH/glycol).\n- **Aldol:** α-H + dil. NaOH → β-hydroxy carbonyl → α,β-unsaturated carbonyl.\n- **Cannizzaro:** NO α-H aldehyde + conc. NaOH → alcohol + carboxylate salt.",
            "bodyHinglish": "- **Nucleophilic Addition Reactivity:** HCHO > RCHO > RCOR' (less steric hindrance + less +I effect).\n- **2,4-DNP test:** Positive for ALL aldehydes & ketones (yellow/orange/red ppt).\n- **Tollens':** ALL aldehydes (silver mirror). **Fehling's:** Aliphatic aldehydes ONLY (red-brown Cu₂O). **Iodoform:** CH₃CO– & CH₃CHO (yellow CHI₃).\n- **>C=O → >CH₂:** Clemmensen (Zn–Hg/HCl) or Wolff–Kishner (NH₂NH₂/KOH/glycol).\n- **Aldol:** α-H + dil. NaOH → β-hydroxy carbonyl → α,β-unsaturated carbonyl.\n- **Cannizzaro:** NO α-H aldehyde + conc. NaOH → alcohol + carboxylate salt."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. Explain why aldehydes are generally more reactive than ketones towards nucleophilic addition reactions (give two reasons).\n2. Arrange ethanal, propanal, propanone, and butanone in increasing order of reactivity towards HCN.\n3. Why is only one of the two –NH₂ groups of semicarbazide (NH₂–NH–CO–NH₂) involved in forming a semicarbazone?\n4. Write the chemical equations for: (a) Clemmensen reduction of propanone, (b) Wolff–Kishner reduction of benzaldehyde.\n5. Write the chemical equations for: (a) Aldol condensation of ethanal (including dehydration step), (b) Cannizzaro reaction of benzaldehyde.\n6. Give ONE simple chemical test to distinguish between:\n   (a) Acetophenone and Benzophenone\n   (b) Pentan-2-one and Pentan-3-one\n   (c) Ethanal and Benzaldehyde\n7. Why is the α-hydrogen of aldehydes and ketones acidic in nature?\n8. Predict the major cross-aldol condensation product of benzaldehyde and ethanal in the presence of dilute NaOH (heated).\n9. What is the product of nitration of benzaldehyde with conc. HNO₃ and conc. H₂SO₄? Why?\n10. Convert: (a) Ethanal to but-2-enal, (b) Ethanal to butane-1,3-diol, (c) Propanone to propene.\n\n---\n\n### Answers\n\n1. (i) **Steric factor:** Two bulky alkyl groups in ketones hinder nucleophile approach more than one alkyl + one small H in aldehydes. (ii) **Electronic factor:** Two +I alkyl groups in ketones reduce the electrophilicity (δ⁺) of the carbonyl carbon more than one alkyl group in aldehydes.\n2. Increasing reactivity: **Butanone < Propanone < Propanal < Ethanal**.\n3. The –NH₂ group directly attached to the carbonyl group (–CO–NH₂) has its lone pair delocalised into C=O by **resonance**, making it non-nucleophilic. Therefore, only the terminal –NH₂ (hydrazine end) attacks the carbonyl compound.\n4. (a) CH₃–CO–CH₃ —(Zn–Hg / conc. HCl)→ **CH₃–CH₂–CH₃ (propane)** + H₂O.\n   (b) C₆H₅–CHO —(1. NH₂NH₂, 2. KOH / ethylene glycol, Δ)→ **C₆H₅–CH₃ (toluene)** + N₂↑.\n5. (a) 2CH₃CHO —(dil. NaOH)→ CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CHO —(Δ, −H₂O)→ **CH₃–CH=CH–CHO (but-2-enal)**.\n   (b) 2C₆H₅CHO + conc. NaOH → **C₆H₅CH₂OH (benzyl alcohol) + C₆H₅COONa (sodium benzoate)**.\n6. (a) **Iodoform test (I₂ + NaOH):** **Acetophenone (C₆H₅COCH₃)** gives yellow CHI₃ ppt; **benzophenone** does not.\n   (b) **Iodoform test (I₂ + NaOH):** **Pentan-2-one** (has CH₃CO–) gives yellow CHI₃ ppt; **pentan-3-one** does not.\n   (c) **Fehling's test (or Iodoform test):** **Ethanal** gives red-brown Cu₂O ppt (and yellow CHI₃ ppt); **benzaldehyde** gives neither!\n7. Because of the strong electron-withdrawing (−I) effect of the adjacent C=O group AND **resonance stabilisation of the conjugate base (enolate ion)** after losing H⁺.\n8. C₆H₅–CHO + CH₃–CHO —(dil. NaOH, Δ)→ **C₆H₅–CH=CH–CHO (cinnamaldehyde / 3-phenylprop-2-enal)** + H₂O.\n9. **3-Nitrobenzaldehyde (m-nitrobenzaldehyde)** because –CHO is electron-withdrawing (−R, −I) and therefore **meta-directing**.\n10. (a) 2CH₃CHO —(dil. NaOH, Δ, Aldol)→ **CH₃–CH=CH–CHO**.\n    (b) 2CH₃CHO —(dil. NaOH)→ CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CHO —(NaBH₄)→ **CH₃–CH(OH)–CH₂–CH₂OH (butane-1,3-diol)**.\n    (c) CH₃COCH₃ —(NaBH₄)→ CH₃CH(OH)CH₃ —(conc. H₂SO₄, 443 K)→ **CH₃–CH=CH₂ (propene)**.\n    Jaanch: All three multi-step routes are standard, high-yield sequences ✅\n\n---\n\nAb tum aldehydes aur ketones ke sabhi core reactions — Aldol, Cannizzaro, Tollens', Fehling's aur Iodoform — par poori command paa chuke ho!\n\n**Agle topic mein:** Hum [Carboxylic Acids: Acidity, Chemical Reactions and Uses](aldehydes-ketones-and-carboxylic-acids-part4.md) padhenge — jahan **HVZ Reaction, Decarboxylation aur Acidity Orders** seekhenge!",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. Explain why aldehydes are generally more reactive than ketones towards nucleophilic addition reactions (give two reasons).\n2. Arrange ethanal, propanal, propanone, and butanone in increasing order of reactivity towards HCN.\n3. Why is only one of the two –NH₂ groups of semicarbazide (NH₂–NH–CO–NH₂) involved in forming a semicarbazone?\n4. Write the chemical equations for: (a) Clemmensen reduction of propanone, (b) Wolff–Kishner reduction of benzaldehyde.\n5. Write the chemical equations for: (a) Aldol condensation of ethanal (including dehydration step), (b) Cannizzaro reaction of benzaldehyde.\n6. Give ONE simple chemical test to distinguish between:\n   (a) Acetophenone and Benzophenone\n   (b) Pentan-2-one and Pentan-3-one\n   (c) Ethanal and Benzaldehyde\n7. Why is the α-hydrogen of aldehydes and ketones acidic in nature?\n8. Predict the major cross-aldol condensation product of benzaldehyde and ethanal in the presence of dilute NaOH (heated).\n9. What is the product of nitration of benzaldehyde with conc. HNO₃ and conc. H₂SO₄? Why?\n10. Convert: (a) Ethanal to but-2-enal, (b) Ethanal to butane-1,3-diol, (c) Propanone to propene.\n\n---\n\n### Answers\n\n1. (i) **Steric factor:** Two bulky alkyl groups in ketones hinder nucleophile approach more than one alkyl + one small H in aldehydes. (ii) **Electronic factor:** Two +I alkyl groups in ketones reduce the electrophilicity (δ⁺) of the carbonyl carbon more than one alkyl group in aldehydes.\n2. Increasing reactivity: **Butanone < Propanone < Propanal < Ethanal**.\n3. The –NH₂ group directly attached to the carbonyl group (–CO–NH₂) has its lone pair delocalised into C=O by **resonance**, making it non-nucleophilic. Therefore, only the terminal –NH₂ (hydrazine end) attacks the carbonyl compound.\n4. (a) CH₃–CO–CH₃ —(Zn–Hg / conc. HCl)→ **CH₃–CH₂–CH₃ (propane)** + H₂O.\n   (b) C₆H₅–CHO —(1. NH₂NH₂, 2. KOH / ethylene glycol, Δ)→ **C₆H₅–CH₃ (toluene)** + N₂↑.\n5. (a) 2CH₃CHO —(dil. NaOH)→ CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CHO —(Δ, −H₂O)→ **CH₃–CH=CH–CHO (but-2-enal)**.\n   (b) 2C₆H₅CHO + conc. NaOH → **C₆H₅CH₂OH (benzyl alcohol) + C₆H₅COONa (sodium benzoate)**.\n6. (a) **Iodoform test (I₂ + NaOH):** **Acetophenone (C₆H₅COCH₃)** gives yellow CHI₃ ppt; **benzophenone** does not.\n   (b) **Iodoform test (I₂ + NaOH):** **Pentan-2-one** (has CH₃CO–) gives yellow CHI₃ ppt; **pentan-3-one** does not.\n   (c) **Fehling's test (or Iodoform test):** **Ethanal** gives red-brown Cu₂O ppt (and yellow CHI₃ ppt); **benzaldehyde** gives neither!\n7. Because of the strong electron-withdrawing (−I) effect of the adjacent C=O group AND **resonance stabilisation of the conjugate base (enolate ion)** after losing H⁺.\n8. C₆H₅–CHO + CH₃–CHO —(dil. NaOH, Δ)→ **C₆H₅–CH=CH–CHO (cinnamaldehyde / 3-phenylprop-2-enal)** + H₂O.\n9. **3-Nitrobenzaldehyde (m-nitrobenzaldehyde)** because –CHO is electron-withdrawing (−R, −I) and therefore **meta-directing**.\n10. (a) 2CH₃CHO —(dil. NaOH, Δ, Aldol)→ **CH₃–CH=CH–CHO**.\n    (b) 2CH₃CHO —(dil. NaOH)→ CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CHO —(NaBH₄)→ **CH₃–CH(OH)–CH₂–CH₂OH (butane-1,3-diol)**.\n    (c) CH₃COCH₃ —(NaBH₄)→ CH₃CH(OH)CH₃ —(conc. H₂SO₄, 443 K)→ **CH₃–CH=CH₂ (propene)**.\n    Jaanch: All three multi-step routes are standard, high-yield sequences ✅\n\n---\n\nAb tum aldehydes aur ketones ke sabhi core reactions — Aldol, Cannizzaro, Tollens', Fehling's aur Iodoform — par poori command paa chuke ho!\n\n**Agle topic mein:** Hum [Carboxylic Acids: Acidity, Chemical Reactions and Uses](aldehydes-ketones-and-carboxylic-acids-part4.md) padhenge — jahan **HVZ Reaction, Decarboxylation aur Acidity Orders** seekhenge!"
          }
