          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- **BP:** 1° > 2° > 3° (more N–H bonds = more H-bonding); Alcohols > Amines.\n- **Basicity:** Gas: 3° > 2° > 1° > NH₃. Aqueous: **Ethyl = 231**, **Methyl = 213**. Aniline << NH₃ (lone pair in ring resonance).\n- **Tests:** **Carbylamine** (CHCl₃/KOH) = 1° ONLY; **Hinsberg** (C₆H₅SO₂Cl) = 1° (KOH-soluble), 2° (KOH-insoluble), 3° (no rxn).\n- **Aniline EAS:** Br₂/H₂O → 2,4,6-tribromoaniline; protect with (CH₃CO)₂O for monobromo/p-nitro; Direct nitration → **47% meta**; Sulphonation → zwitterionic sulphanilic acid; **NO Friedel–Crafts** (salt with AlCl₃!).",
            "bodyHinglish": "- **BP:** 1° > 2° > 3° (more N–H bonds = more H-bonding); Alcohols > Amines.\n- **Basicity:** Gas: 3° > 2° > 1° > NH₃. Aqueous: **Ethyl = 231**, **Methyl = 213**. Aniline << NH₃ (lone pair in ring resonance).\n- **Tests:** **Carbylamine** (CHCl₃/KOH) = 1° ONLY; **Hinsberg** (C₆H₅SO₂Cl) = 1° (KOH-soluble), 2° (KOH-insoluble), 3° (no rxn).\n- **Aniline EAS:** Br₂/H₂O → 2,4,6-tribromoaniline; protect with (CH₃CO)₂O for monobromo/p-nitro; Direct nitration → **47% meta**; Sulphonation → zwitterionic sulphanilic acid; **NO Friedel–Crafts** (salt with AlCl₃!)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. Explain why primary amines have higher boiling points than tertiary amines of comparable molar mass.\n2. Explain why the basicity order of methylamines in aqueous solution is (CH₃)₂NH > CH₃NH₂ > (CH₃)₃N > NH₃, whereas in gas phase it is (CH₃)₃N > (CH₃)₂NH > CH₃NH₂ > NH₃.\n3. Why is aniline a much weaker base than cyclohexylamine (or ethylamine)?\n4. Arrange in increasing order of basic strength in water: Aniline, p-Nitroaniline, p-Toluidine.\n5. Write the chemical equation for the **Carbylamine reaction** of aniline. Which class of amines responds to this test?\n6. Explain how **Hinsberg's reagent (C₆H₅SO₂Cl)** is used to distinguish between 1°, 2°, and 3° amines.\n7. Why does direct nitration of aniline give a surprisingly large amount (47%) of m-nitroaniline? How can you prepare pure p-nitroaniline from aniline?\n8. Why does aniline NOT undergo Friedel–Crafts alkylation or acylation?\n9. Write the structure of **sulphanilic acid** in its zwitterionic form.\n10. Give ONE chemical test to distinguish between:\n    (a) Aniline and N-Methylaniline\n    (b) Aniline and Benzylamine\n\n---\n\n### Answers\n\n1. 1° amines (R–NH₂) have two N–H bonds capable of forming extensive **intermolecular hydrogen bonds**, whereas 3° amines (R₃N) have no N–H bonds and cannot self-hydrogen bond, resulting in much lower boiling points.\n2. In the **gas phase**, only the +I inductive effect operates → 3° > 2° > 1° > NH₃. In **aqueous solution**, stability of the conjugate acid depends on **+I effect, hydration (H-bonding with H₂O: 1° > 2° > 3°), and steric hindrance**. For small methyl groups, hydration favours 1° over 3°, giving **2° > 1° > 3° > NH₃** (\"213\").\n3. In aniline, nitrogen's lone pair is **delocalised into the benzene ring by resonance**, making it much less available to accept a proton. In cyclohexylamine/ethylamine, the lone pair is localised on N and enhanced by the +I alkyl group.\n4. Increasing basic strength: **p-Nitroaniline < Aniline < p-Toluidine** (–NO₂ withdraws electrons, –CH₃ donates electrons).\n5. **C₆H₅–NH₂ + CHCl₃ + 3KOH (alc., Δ) → C₆H₅–NC (phenyl isocyanide, foul smell) + 3KCl + 3H₂O**. Only **1° amines** (both aliphatic and aromatic) respond.\n6. React with **C₆H₅SO₂Cl**:\n   - **1° amine** → C₆H₅SO₂NHR, **soluble in aq. KOH** (acidic N–H).\n   - **2° amine** → C₆H₅SO₂NR₂, **insoluble in aq. KOH** (no N–H).\n   - **3° amine** → **no reaction** with C₆H₅SO₂Cl.\n7. In strongly acidic nitrating mixture, aniline is protonated to the **anilinium ion (C₆H₅–NH₃⁺)**, which is **meta-directing**, producing 47% m-nitroaniline. To get pure **p-nitroaniline**, first **acetylate** aniline with (CH₃CO)₂O/pyridine to acetanilide, nitrate with conc. HNO₃/H₂SO₄, and then hydrolyse (H⁺/H₂O)!\n8. Because aniline is a Lewis base that forms a **salt with the Lewis acid catalyst AlCl₃** (C₆H₅–NH₂⁺–AlCl₃⁻), imparting a positive charge on nitrogen that strongly deactivates the benzene ring.\n9. **⁺H₃N–C₆H₄–SO₃⁻** (para positions on benzene ring).\n10. (a) **Carbylamine test (CHCl₃ + alc. KOH):** **Aniline (1°)** gives foul-smelling phenyl isocyanide; **N-methylaniline (2°)** gives NO reaction.\n    (b) **Nitrous acid (NaNO₂ + HCl, 0–5°C) + β-naphthol:** **Aniline** forms a brilliant **orange-red azo dye**; **benzylamine** (1° aliphatic-like) evolves N₂ gas forming benzyl alcohol (no dye!).\n\n---\n\nAb tum amines ki basicity, Hinsberg/Carbylamine tests aur aniline ke saare tricky EAS reactions master kar chuke ho!\n\n**Agle topic mein:** Hum [Diazonium Salts: Preparation, Reactions and Importance](amines-part4.md) padhenge — jahan ek single compound (C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻) benzene ring par koi bhi group (Cl, Br, I, F, CN, OH, H, NO₂, aur colourful Azo Dyes!) laga sakta hai!",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. Explain why primary amines have higher boiling points than tertiary amines of comparable molar mass.\n2. Explain why the basicity order of methylamines in aqueous solution is (CH₃)₂NH > CH₃NH₂ > (CH₃)₃N > NH₃, whereas in gas phase it is (CH₃)₃N > (CH₃)₂NH > CH₃NH₂ > NH₃.\n3. Why is aniline a much weaker base than cyclohexylamine (or ethylamine)?\n4. Arrange in increasing order of basic strength in water: Aniline, p-Nitroaniline, p-Toluidine.\n5. Write the chemical equation for the **Carbylamine reaction** of aniline. Which class of amines responds to this test?\n6. Explain how **Hinsberg's reagent (C₆H₅SO₂Cl)** is used to distinguish between 1°, 2°, and 3° amines.\n7. Why does direct nitration of aniline give a surprisingly large amount (47%) of m-nitroaniline? How can you prepare pure p-nitroaniline from aniline?\n8. Why does aniline NOT undergo Friedel–Crafts alkylation or acylation?\n9. Write the structure of **sulphanilic acid** in its zwitterionic form.\n10. Give ONE chemical test to distinguish between:\n    (a) Aniline and N-Methylaniline\n    (b) Aniline and Benzylamine\n\n---\n\n### Answers\n\n1. 1° amines (R–NH₂) have two N–H bonds capable of forming extensive **intermolecular hydrogen bonds**, whereas 3° amines (R₃N) have no N–H bonds and cannot self-hydrogen bond, resulting in much lower boiling points.\n2. In the **gas phase**, only the +I inductive effect operates → 3° > 2° > 1° > NH₃. In **aqueous solution**, stability of the conjugate acid depends on **+I effect, hydration (H-bonding with H₂O: 1° > 2° > 3°), and steric hindrance**. For small methyl groups, hydration favours 1° over 3°, giving **2° > 1° > 3° > NH₃** (\"213\").\n3. In aniline, nitrogen's lone pair is **delocalised into the benzene ring by resonance**, making it much less available to accept a proton. In cyclohexylamine/ethylamine, the lone pair is localised on N and enhanced by the +I alkyl group.\n4. Increasing basic strength: **p-Nitroaniline < Aniline < p-Toluidine** (–NO₂ withdraws electrons, –CH₃ donates electrons).\n5. **C₆H₅–NH₂ + CHCl₃ + 3KOH (alc., Δ) → C₆H₅–NC (phenyl isocyanide, foul smell) + 3KCl + 3H₂O**. Only **1° amines** (both aliphatic and aromatic) respond.\n6. React with **C₆H₅SO₂Cl**:\n   - **1° amine** → C₆H₅SO₂NHR, **soluble in aq. KOH** (acidic N–H).\n   - **2° amine** → C₆H₅SO₂NR₂, **insoluble in aq. KOH** (no N–H).\n   - **3° amine** → **no reaction** with C₆H₅SO₂Cl.\n7. In strongly acidic nitrating mixture, aniline is protonated to the **anilinium ion (C₆H₅–NH₃⁺)**, which is **meta-directing**, producing 47% m-nitroaniline. To get pure **p-nitroaniline**, first **acetylate** aniline with (CH₃CO)₂O/pyridine to acetanilide, nitrate with conc. HNO₃/H₂SO₄, and then hydrolyse (H⁺/H₂O)!\n8. Because aniline is a Lewis base that forms a **salt with the Lewis acid catalyst AlCl₃** (C₆H₅–NH₂⁺–AlCl₃⁻), imparting a positive charge on nitrogen that strongly deactivates the benzene ring.\n9. **⁺H₃N–C₆H₄–SO₃⁻** (para positions on benzene ring).\n10. (a) **Carbylamine test (CHCl₃ + alc. KOH):** **Aniline (1°)** gives foul-smelling phenyl isocyanide; **N-methylaniline (2°)** gives NO reaction.\n    (b) **Nitrous acid (NaNO₂ + HCl, 0–5°C) + β-naphthol:** **Aniline** forms a brilliant **orange-red azo dye**; **benzylamine** (1° aliphatic-like) evolves N₂ gas forming benzyl alcohol (no dye!).\n\n---\n\nAb tum amines ki basicity, Hinsberg/Carbylamine tests aur aniline ke saare tricky EAS reactions master kar chuke ho!\n\n**Agle topic mein:** Hum [Diazonium Salts: Preparation, Reactions and Importance](amines-part4.md) padhenge — jahan ek single compound (C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻) benzene ring par koi bhi group (Cl, Br, I, F, CN, OH, H, NO₂, aur colourful Azo Dyes!) laga sakta hai!"
          }
