          {
            "heading": "1. Amino Acids — Classification, Optical Activity & Zwitterion",
            "headingHinglish": "1. Amino Acids — Classification, Optical Activity & Zwitterion",
            "body": "Proteins ke hydrolysis se **20 naturally occurring α-amino acids** milte hain (sabhi natural amino acids **L-configuration** mein hote hain!).\n\n### (a) Classification of Amino Acids:\n1. **By Acidic / Basic / Neutral Nature:**\n   - **Neutral:** Equal –NH₂ and –COOH (1 each) — e.g., **Glycine, Alanine, Valine**.\n   - **Acidic:** More –COOH than –NH₂ — e.g., **Aspartic acid, Glutamic acid**.\n   - **Basic:** More –NH₂ than –COOH — e.g., **Lysine, Arginine, Histidine**.\n2. **By Dietary Need:**\n   - **Non-essential (10):** Body can synthesise them (e.g., Glycine, Alanine, Glutamic acid).\n   - **Essential (10):** Must be obtained from diet (e.g., Valine, Leucine, Isoleucine, Lysine, Phenylalanine, Tryptophan).\n\n### (b) Optical Activity Exception (100% Exam Question!):\nSabhi natural α-amino acids **optically active (chiral)** hote hain — **SIWAYE GLYCINE (H₂N–CH₂–COOH) ke!**\nKyun? Kyunki glycine ke α-carbon par **do identical H-atoms** jude hain (achiral / no asymmetric carbon)!\n\n### (c) Zwitterion & Amphoteric Behaviour:\nAqueous solution aur solid state mein –COOH apna proton (H⁺) –NH₂ ko de deta hai → **Zwitterion (dipolar ion)**:\n**R–CH(NH₂)–COOH  ⇌  R–CH(⁺NH₃)–COO⁻**\n- **Why do amino acids have high melting points and behave like salts?** Because they exist as strongly attracted electrostatic **zwitterions**!\n- **Amphoteric nature:**\n  - Acidic medium (low pH) mein: COO⁻ proton lekar **cation** (⁺H₃N–CH(R)–COOH) ban jata hai.\n  - Basic medium (high pH) mein: ⁺NH₃ proton dekar **anion** (H₂N–CH(R)–COO⁻) ban jata hai.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Proteins ke hydrolysis se **20 naturally occurring α-amino acids** milte hain (sabhi natural amino acids **L-configuration** mein hote hain!).\n\n### (a) Classification of Amino Acids:\n1. **By Acidic / Basic / Neutral Nature:**\n   - **Neutral:** Equal –NH₂ and –COOH (1 each) — e.g., **Glycine, Alanine, Valine**.\n   - **Acidic:** More –COOH than –NH₂ — e.g., **Aspartic acid, Glutamic acid**.\n   - **Basic:** More –NH₂ than –COOH — e.g., **Lysine, Arginine, Histidine**.\n2. **By Dietary Need:**\n   - **Non-essential (10):** Body can synthesise them (e.g., Glycine, Alanine, Glutamic acid).\n   - **Essential (10):** Must be obtained from diet (e.g., Valine, Leucine, Isoleucine, Lysine, Phenylalanine, Tryptophan).\n\n### (b) Optical Activity Exception (100% Exam Question!):\nSabhi natural α-amino acids **optically active (chiral)** hote hain — **SIWAYE GLYCINE (H₂N–CH₂–COOH) ke!**\nKyun? Kyunki glycine ke α-carbon par **do identical H-atoms** jude hain (achiral / no asymmetric carbon)!\n\n### (c) Zwitterion & Amphoteric Behaviour:\nAqueous solution aur solid state mein –COOH apna proton (H⁺) –NH₂ ko de deta hai → **Zwitterion (dipolar ion)**:\n**R–CH(NH₂)–COOH  ⇌  R–CH(⁺NH₃)–COO⁻**\n- **Why do amino acids have high melting points and behave like salts?** Because they exist as strongly attracted electrostatic **zwitterions**!\n- **Amphoteric nature:**\n  - Acidic medium (low pH) mein: COO⁻ proton lekar **cation** (⁺H₃N–CH(R)–COOH) ban jata hai.\n  - Basic medium (high pH) mein: ⁺NH₃ proton dekar **anion** (H₂N–CH(R)–COO⁻) ban jata hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "2. Peptide Bond & Polypeptides",
            "headingHinglish": "2. Peptide Bond & Polypeptides",
            "body": "Jab do amino acids (e.g., Glycine + Alanine) judte hain, toh ek ka –COOH aur doosre ka –NH₂ milkar H₂O nikalte hain aur **Peptide Bond (–CO–NH–)** banta hai!\n- **2 amino acids → Dipeptide** (**1** peptide bond) — e.g., Glycylalanine (Gly–Ala).\n- **3 amino acids → Tripeptide** (**2** peptide bonds).\n- **n amino acids → Polypeptide** (**n − 1** peptide bonds!).\n- **Protein:** Polypeptide with **> 100 amino acid residues** and molecular mass **> 10,000 u** (Exception: **Insulin** mein sirf **51 amino acids** hain, phir bhi use protein kehte hain!).",
            "bodyHinglish": "Jab do amino acids (e.g., Glycine + Alanine) judte hain, toh ek ka –COOH aur doosre ka –NH₂ milkar H₂O nikalte hain aur **Peptide Bond (–CO–NH–)** banta hai!\n- **2 amino acids → Dipeptide** (**1** peptide bond) — e.g., Glycylalanine (Gly–Ala).\n- **3 amino acids → Tripeptide** (**2** peptide bonds).\n- **n amino acids → Polypeptide** (**n − 1** peptide bonds!).\n- **Protein:** Polypeptide with **> 100 amino acid residues** and molecular mass **> 10,000 u** (Exception: **Insulin** mein sirf **51 amino acids** hain, phir bhi use protein kehte hain!)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "3. Classification & Four Levels of Protein Structure",
            "headingHinglish": "3. Classification & Four Levels of Protein Structure",
            "body": "### (a) Fibrous vs Globular Proteins:\n| Feature | Fibrous Proteins | Globular Proteins |\n|---|---|---|\n| Shape | Parallel polypeptide chains forming **fibre-like / thread-like** structure | Polypeptide chains coil into **spherical / globular** shape |\n| Bonds holding chains | Hydrogen bonds and **disulphide (–S–S–)** bonds | H-bonds, disulphide, electrostatic & van der Waals |\n| Water solubility | **INSOLUBLE** in water | **SOLUBLE** in water |\n| Examples | **Keratin** (hair, wool, silk), **Myosin** (muscles) | **Insulin**, **Albumins** (egg white), **Haemoglobin** |\n\n### (b) Four Structural Levels of Proteins:\n1. **Primary (1°) Structure:** Polypeptide chain mein **amino acids ka exact linear sequence** (peptide bonds se juda). Ek bhi amino acid badalne se alag protein ban jata hai (jaise sickle-cell anaemia!).\n2. **Secondary (2°) Structure:** Polypeptide backbone ka regular local folding (**intramolecular / intermolecular H-bonds** between C=O and N–H of peptide bonds):\n   - **α-Helix:** Right-handed screw/spiral stabilised by **intramolecular H-bonds** between C=O of one turn and N–H of adjacent turn (e.g., keratin in hair/wool).\n   - **β-Pleated Sheet:** Stretched chains lying side-by-side held by **intermolecular H-bonds** (e.g., silk fibroin).\n3. **Tertiary (3°) Structure:** 2° structure ka overall **3D folding** (gives fibrous or globular shape). Stabilised by **4 forces:** Hydrogen bonds, **Disulphide linkages (–S–S–)**, van der Waals forces, and **Electrostatic (salt-bridge)** attractions.\n4. **Quaternary (4°) Structure:** Do ya zyada polypeptide chains (**subunits**) ka ek saath spatial arrangement (e.g., Haemoglobin has 4 subunits!).",
            "bodyHinglish": "### (a) Fibrous vs Globular Proteins:\n| Feature | Fibrous Proteins | Globular Proteins |\n|---|---|---|\n| Shape | Parallel polypeptide chains forming **fibre-like / thread-like** structure | Polypeptide chains coil into **spherical / globular** shape |\n| Bonds holding chains | Hydrogen bonds and **disulphide (–S–S–)** bonds | H-bonds, disulphide, electrostatic & van der Waals |\n| Water solubility | **INSOLUBLE** in water | **SOLUBLE** in water |\n| Examples | **Keratin** (hair, wool, silk), **Myosin** (muscles) | **Insulin**, **Albumins** (egg white), **Haemoglobin** |\n\n### (b) Four Structural Levels of Proteins:\n1. **Primary (1°) Structure:** Polypeptide chain mein **amino acids ka exact linear sequence** (peptide bonds se juda). Ek bhi amino acid badalne se alag protein ban jata hai (jaise sickle-cell anaemia!).\n2. **Secondary (2°) Structure:** Polypeptide backbone ka regular local folding (**intramolecular / intermolecular H-bonds** between C=O and N–H of peptide bonds):\n   - **α-Helix:** Right-handed screw/spiral stabilised by **intramolecular H-bonds** between C=O of one turn and N–H of adjacent turn (e.g., keratin in hair/wool).\n   - **β-Pleated Sheet:** Stretched chains lying side-by-side held by **intermolecular H-bonds** (e.g., silk fibroin).\n3. **Tertiary (3°) Structure:** 2° structure ka overall **3D folding** (gives fibrous or globular shape). Stabilised by **4 forces:** Hydrogen bonds, **Disulphide linkages (–S–S–)**, van der Waals forces, and **Electrostatic (salt-bridge)** attractions.\n4. **Quaternary (4°) Structure:** Do ya zyada polypeptide chains (**subunits**) ka ek saath spatial arrangement (e.g., Haemoglobin has 4 subunits!)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "4. Denaturation of Proteins",
            "headingHinglish": "4. Denaturation of Proteins",
            "body": "Jab native protein ko **physical change (temperature/heat)** ya **chemical change (pH/acid/base)** diya jata hai:\n- Hydrogen bonds aur non-covalent forces disturb hokar **globules uncoil** aur **helices unfold** ho jati hain!\n- Protein apni **biological activity kho deta hai** (**Denaturation**)!\n- **CRITICAL EXAM FACT:** Denaturation mein **2°, 3°, aur 4° structures destroy ho jati hain**, lekin **1° (PRIMARY) STRUCTURE (peptide bonds / amino-acid sequence) BILKUL INTACT REHTI HAI!**\n- **Common Examples:** (i) **Coagulation of egg white on boiling**, (ii) **Curdling of milk** (bacteria producing lactic acid lowers pH!).",
            "bodyHinglish": "Jab native protein ko **physical change (temperature/heat)** ya **chemical change (pH/acid/base)** diya jata hai:\n- Hydrogen bonds aur non-covalent forces disturb hokar **globules uncoil** aur **helices unfold** ho jati hain!\n- Protein apni **biological activity kho deta hai** (**Denaturation**)!\n- **CRITICAL EXAM FACT:** Denaturation mein **2°, 3°, aur 4° structures destroy ho jati hain**, lekin **1° (PRIMARY) STRUCTURE (peptide bonds / amino-acid sequence) BILKUL INTACT REHTI HAI!**\n- **Common Examples:** (i) **Coagulation of egg white on boiling**, (ii) **Curdling of milk** (bacteria producing lactic acid lowers pH!)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Solved Examples",
            "headingHinglish": "Solved Examples",
            "body": "### Example 1: Counting Peptide Bonds and Possible Tripeptides\n(a) How many peptide bonds are present in a **tetrapeptide**?\n(b) How many different **tripeptides** can be formed using three different amino acids Gly, Ala, and Val (using each once)?\n\n**Solution:**\n(a) An n-peptide has **(n − 1)** peptide bonds. For a tetrapeptide (n = 4): 4 − 1 = **3 peptide bonds**.\n(b) With 3 distinct amino acids used once each, total arrangements = 3! = 3 × 2 × 1 = **6 tripeptides** (Gly–Ala–Val, Gly–Val–Ala, Ala–Gly–Val, Ala–Val–Gly, Val–Gly–Ala, Val–Ala–Gly).\n\nJaanch: 4 residues linked linearly require 3 links; 3! = 6 distinct sequences ✅\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Explaining Water Solubility of Amino Acids\nWhy do amino acids behave like crystalline salts (high melting points, soluble in water) rather than simple amines or carboxylic acids?\n\n**Solution:**\nIn the solid state and aqueous solution, amino acids undergo internal proton transfer from –COOH to –NH₂ to form dipolar **zwitterions (⁺H₃N–CH(R)–COO⁻)**. Strong electrostatic (ionic) attractions between oppositely charged ends give high melting points and high water solubility (strong ion–dipole hydration), just like inorganic salts!\n\nJaanch: Dipolar ionic lattice = salt-like high MP and water solubility ✅\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: What Happens to Water During Boiling of an Egg?\nWhere does the water present in a raw egg go after boiling the egg?\n\n**Solution:**\nDuring boiling, the globular proteins in egg white undergo **denaturation** (unfolding and coagulation into a 3D network). The water molecules present in the egg get **absorbed / trapped inside this coagulated protein network through hydrogen bonding**!\n\nJaanch: No water evaporates through the sealed shell — it is H-bonded into the denatured protein gel ✅",
            "bodyHinglish": "### Example 1: Counting Peptide Bonds and Possible Tripeptides\n(a) How many peptide bonds are present in a **tetrapeptide**?\n(b) How many different **tripeptides** can be formed using three different amino acids Gly, Ala, and Val (using each once)?\n\n**Solution:**\n(a) An n-peptide has **(n − 1)** peptide bonds. For a tetrapeptide (n = 4): 4 − 1 = **3 peptide bonds**.\n(b) With 3 distinct amino acids used once each, total arrangements = 3! = 3 × 2 × 1 = **6 tripeptides** (Gly–Ala–Val, Gly–Val–Ala, Ala–Gly–Val, Ala–Val–Gly, Val–Gly–Ala, Val–Ala–Gly).\n\nJaanch: 4 residues linked linearly require 3 links; 3! = 6 distinct sequences ✅\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Explaining Water Solubility of Amino Acids\nWhy do amino acids behave like crystalline salts (high melting points, soluble in water) rather than simple amines or carboxylic acids?\n\n**Solution:**\nIn the solid state and aqueous solution, amino acids undergo internal proton transfer from –COOH to –NH₂ to form dipolar **zwitterions (⁺H₃N–CH(R)–COO⁻)**. Strong electrostatic (ionic) attractions between oppositely charged ends give high melting points and high water solubility (strong ion–dipole hydration), just like inorganic salts!\n\nJaanch: Dipolar ionic lattice = salt-like high MP and water solubility ✅\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: What Happens to Water During Boiling of an Egg?\nWhere does the water present in a raw egg go after boiling the egg?\n\n**Solution:**\nDuring boiling, the globular proteins in egg white undergo **denaturation** (unfolding and coagulation into a 3D network). The water molecules present in the egg get **absorbed / trapped inside this coagulated protein network through hydrogen bonding**!\n\nJaanch: No water evaporates through the sealed shell — it is H-bonded into the denatured protein gel ✅"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Common Exam Mistakes (Watch Out!)",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam Galtiyan (Common Mistakes)",
            "body": "1. **Sabhi natural amino acids ko optically active bol dena:**\n   - *Galat:* Saare 20 α-amino acids optically active hain.\n   - *Sahi:* **Glycine (H₂N–CH₂–COOH)** achiral aur **optically INACTIVE** hai! Baaki 19 optically active hain.\n\n2. **Denaturation mein primary structure ko bhi tootna bol dena:**\n   - *Galat:* Ubalne par protein ke peptide bonds toot jate hain.\n   - *Sahi:* Denaturation mein **sirf 2°, 3°, aur 4° structures** toot-ti hain; **1° structure (covalent peptide bonds) intact** rehti hai!\n\n3. **Tripeptide mein 3 peptide bonds gin lena:**\n   - *Galat:* Tripeptide = 3 peptide bonds.\n   - *Sahi:* Tripeptide = **3 amino acids** + **2 peptide bonds** (hamesha n − 1)!",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. **Sabhi natural amino acids ko optically active bol dena:**\n   - *Galat:* Saare 20 α-amino acids optically active hain.\n   - *Sahi:* **Glycine (H₂N–CH₂–COOH)** achiral aur **optically INACTIVE** hai! Baaki 19 optically active hain.\n\n2. **Denaturation mein primary structure ko bhi tootna bol dena:**\n   - *Galat:* Ubalne par protein ke peptide bonds toot jate hain.\n   - *Sahi:* Denaturation mein **sirf 2°, 3°, aur 4° structures** toot-ti hain; **1° structure (covalent peptide bonds) intact** rehti hai!\n\n3. **Tripeptide mein 3 peptide bonds gin lena:**\n   - *Galat:* Tripeptide = 3 peptide bonds.\n   - *Sahi:* Tripeptide = **3 amino acids** + **2 peptide bonds** (hamesha n − 1)!"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- **Amino Acids:** 20 α-amino acids (all L-config; all chiral EXCEPT **Glycine**); exist as amphoteric **zwitterions** (⁺H₃N–CHR–COO⁻); 10 essential (from diet).\n- **Peptide bond:** –CO–NH– (n residues → n − 1 peptide bonds).\n- **Fibrous** (keratin, myosin — insoluble) vs **Globular** (insulin, albumin — soluble).\n- **Structures:** 1° (sequence), 2° (α-helix & β-pleated sheet via H-bonds), 3° (3D folding via H-bond, –S–S–, electrostatic, vdW), 4° (subunits).\n- **Denaturation:** Heat/pH destroys 2°, 3°, 4° & biological activity; **1° stays intact** (e.g., boiling egg, curdling milk).",
            "bodyHinglish": "- **Amino Acids:** 20 α-amino acids (all L-config; all chiral EXCEPT **Glycine**); exist as amphoteric **zwitterions** (⁺H₃N–CHR–COO⁻); 10 essential (from diet).\n- **Peptide bond:** –CO–NH– (n residues → n − 1 peptide bonds).\n- **Fibrous** (keratin, myosin — insoluble) vs **Globular** (insulin, albumin — soluble).\n- **Structures:** 1° (sequence), 2° (α-helix & β-pleated sheet via H-bonds), 3° (3D folding via H-bond, –S–S–, electrostatic, vdW), 4° (subunits).\n- **Denaturation:** Heat/pH destroys 2°, 3°, 4° & biological activity; **1° stays intact** (e.g., boiling egg, curdling milk)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. Distinguish between essential and non-essential amino acids with two examples each.\n2. Name the only naturally occurring α-amino acid that is optically inactive, and explain why.\n3. What is a zwitterion? Write the zwitterionic structure of alanine (CH₃–CH(NH₂)–COOH) and explain its amphoteric behaviour.\n4. Define a peptide linkage. How many peptide bonds are present in a pentapeptide?\n5. Differentiate between fibrous proteins and globular proteins (any two points + one example each).\n6. What forces stabilise the secondary (2°) and tertiary (3°) structures of proteins?\n7. Distinguish between α-helix and β-pleated sheet structures of proteins.\n8. What is denaturation of proteins? Which structural level(s) are destroyed and which level remains intact during denaturation?\n9. Give two everyday examples of denaturation of proteins.\n10. Why do amino acids have much higher melting points than corresponding haloacids of similar molecular mass?\n\n---\n\n### Answers\n\n1. **Essential amino acids** cannot be synthesised in the body and must be obtained from diet (e.g., valine, leucine, lysine). **Non-essential amino acids** are synthesised by the body (e.g., glycine, alanine).\n2. **Glycine (H₂N–CH₂–COOH)** — because its α-carbon is bonded to two identical hydrogen atoms (not chiral).\n3. A **zwitterion** is a neutral dipolar ion containing both positive and negative charges on the same molecule: **CH₃–CH(⁺NH₃)–COO⁻**. It is amphoteric because –COO⁻ accepts H⁺ in acid (forming a cation) while –⁺NH₃ donates H⁺ in base (forming an anion).\n4. A **peptide linkage (–CO–NH–)** is an amide bond formed between the –COOH of one amino acid and the –NH₂ of another with elimination of H₂O. A pentapeptide (5 amino acids) has 5 − 1 = **4 peptide bonds**.\n5. **Fibrous:** Thread-like parallel chains, **insoluble** in water (e.g., keratin, myosin). **Globular:** Folded spherical chains, **soluble** in water (e.g., insulin, albumin).\n6. **Secondary (2°):** Hydrogen bonds between C=O and N–H of the peptide backbone. **Tertiary (3°):** Hydrogen bonds, **disulphide linkages (–S–S–)**, van der Waals forces, and electrostatic attractions.\n7. **α-Helix** is a right-handed spiral stabilised by **intramolecular H-bonds** along the same chain (e.g., wool/hair keratin). **β-Pleated sheet** consists of stretched chains held side-by-side by **intermolecular H-bonds** (e.g., silk).\n8. **Denaturation** is the loss of biological activity and 3D folding of a native protein due to heat or pH change. **2°, 3°, and 4° structures** are destroyed, while the **primary (1°) structure remains completely intact**.\n9. (i) **Coagulation of egg white** on boiling; (ii) **Curdling of milk** by lactic acid.\n10. Because amino acids exist as **zwitterions** held together by strong electrostatic (ionic) forces in the solid state, whereas haloacids are neutral covalent molecules held only by weaker H-bonds/dipole forces.\n\n---\n\nAb tum proteins ke amino acids, peptide bonds, 4 structural levels aur denaturation ko bilkul clear samajh chuke ho!\n\n**Agle topic mein:** Hum [Enzymes, Vitamins and Nucleic Acids (DNA and RNA)](biomolecules-part3.md) padhenge — jahan vitamins ki kami se hone wali bimariyan aur DNA double helix ke base-pairing rules seekhenge!",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. Distinguish between essential and non-essential amino acids with two examples each.\n2. Name the only naturally occurring α-amino acid that is optically inactive, and explain why.\n3. What is a zwitterion? Write the zwitterionic structure of alanine (CH₃–CH(NH₂)–COOH) and explain its amphoteric behaviour.\n4. Define a peptide linkage. How many peptide bonds are present in a pentapeptide?\n5. Differentiate between fibrous proteins and globular proteins (any two points + one example each).\n6. What forces stabilise the secondary (2°) and tertiary (3°) structures of proteins?\n7. Distinguish between α-helix and β-pleated sheet structures of proteins.\n8. What is denaturation of proteins? Which structural level(s) are destroyed and which level remains intact during denaturation?\n9. Give two everyday examples of denaturation of proteins.\n10. Why do amino acids have much higher melting points than corresponding haloacids of similar molecular mass?\n\n---\n\n### Answers\n\n1. **Essential amino acids** cannot be synthesised in the body and must be obtained from diet (e.g., valine, leucine, lysine). **Non-essential amino acids** are synthesised by the body (e.g., glycine, alanine).\n2. **Glycine (H₂N–CH₂–COOH)** — because its α-carbon is bonded to two identical hydrogen atoms (not chiral).\n3. A **zwitterion** is a neutral dipolar ion containing both positive and negative charges on the same molecule: **CH₃–CH(⁺NH₃)–COO⁻**. It is amphoteric because –COO⁻ accepts H⁺ in acid (forming a cation) while –⁺NH₃ donates H⁺ in base (forming an anion).\n4. A **peptide linkage (–CO–NH–)** is an amide bond formed between the –COOH of one amino acid and the –NH₂ of another with elimination of H₂O. A pentapeptide (5 amino acids) has 5 − 1 = **4 peptide bonds**.\n5. **Fibrous:** Thread-like parallel chains, **insoluble** in water (e.g., keratin, myosin). **Globular:** Folded spherical chains, **soluble** in water (e.g., insulin, albumin).\n6. **Secondary (2°):** Hydrogen bonds between C=O and N–H of the peptide backbone. **Tertiary (3°):** Hydrogen bonds, **disulphide linkages (–S–S–)**, van der Waals forces, and electrostatic attractions.\n7. **α-Helix** is a right-handed spiral stabilised by **intramolecular H-bonds** along the same chain (e.g., wool/hair keratin). **β-Pleated sheet** consists of stretched chains held side-by-side by **intermolecular H-bonds** (e.g., silk).\n8. **Denaturation** is the loss of biological activity and 3D folding of a native protein due to heat or pH change. **2°, 3°, and 4° structures** are destroyed, while the **primary (1°) structure remains completely intact**.\n9. (i) **Coagulation of egg white** on boiling; (ii) **Curdling of milk** by lactic acid.\n10. Because amino acids exist as **zwitterions** held together by strong electrostatic (ionic) forces in the solid state, whereas haloacids are neutral covalent molecules held only by weaker H-bonds/dipole forces.\n\n---\n\nAb tum proteins ke amino acids, peptide bonds, 4 structural levels aur denaturation ko bilkul clear samajh chuke ho!\n\n**Agle topic mein:** Hum [Enzymes, Vitamins and Nucleic Acids (DNA and RNA)](biomolecules-part3.md) padhenge — jahan vitamins ki kami se hone wali bimariyan aur DNA double helix ke base-pairing rules seekhenge!"
          }
